## 1. The standard and its terms

- **Which:** American Association of School Librarians, National School Library Standards, second edition,
  learner framework (file dated 2025-09-18; the PDF footer says copyright 2026; ALA lists the edition as 2025). Confirmed 2026-11-16 at standards.aasl.org, which presents the "Second
  Edition". Six Shared Foundations (I Inquire, II Include, III Collaborate, IV Curate, V Explore, VI Engage), each in
  four Domains (A Think, B Create, C Share, D Grow): 68 competencies, ids `AASL.I.A.1` style, unchanged from 2018.
- **Name lags (not this subject's files to change):** the file name aasl-2018.txt and the tag `AASL-2018` in
  scripts/standards.py and data/modules.csv stay as names (the file header says why). data/subjects.csv already
  labels the row with the second edition.
- **Terms** (standards.aasl.org/permissions, read 2026-11-16): one competency may be excerpted into a lesson or unit
  plan for non-commercial education; the whole framework or a substantial part needs ALA permission. So the repo holds
  ids and our own words, never the competency text, and pages never claim AASL alignment approved by anyone.
- **Our own words:** data/standards/aasl-words.txt, the id, a tab, and one skill phrase of 8 to 15 words (an
  action and an object, not a rewording of the sentence), for example `AASL.IV.B.3`, "Question each source's maker
  and purpose before trusting what it says." Written without asking ALA for permission, since a phrase names the
  skill and follows no sentence (Cory, 2026-11-21). All 68 written 2026-11-21 against the second edition chart, read
  once from a copy kept outside the repo (S:\CurioClass-sources\library\aasl-2026-11-21; Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
  q-fix-library-1).
- **What it lacks:** AASL competencies are not set by grade. The same 68 apply K to 12. The bar's criterion 2 asks for
  named priority skills per grade, so a grade sequence must come from elsewhere.

## 2. The grade sequence: the Empire State Information Fluency Continuum as the check set

- **What it is:** a PK to 12 continuum by Barbara Stripling for New York City and New York State school libraries,
  hosted by Monroe One BOCES School Library System (libguides.monroe.edu/ESIFC; band files dated 2019; continuum page
  last updated 2019-10-23). Four anchor standards: inquiry and design thinking, multiple literacies, social and civic
  responsibility, personal growth and agency (the guide's framework page, read 2026-11-21; no author named there).
  Six phases inside the inquiry standard (connect, wonder, investigate, construct, express, reflect), confirmed in
  all four band files 2026-11-21, followed by a design thinking standard inside the same anchor. Each phase has
  indicators with a cell per grade column, the priority skills marked in bold in the cells and gathered in a
  "priority skills" box per phase for the whole band.
- **Grade columns, by band file** (checked 2026-11-21): PK to 2 has PK, K, 1 and 2 (no Curio Class course uses the
  PK column); 3 to 5 has 3, 4 and 5; 6 to 8 has 6, 7 and 8; 9 to 12 has only two columns, 9-10 and 11-12. So a
  grade 9 or 10 progression row is checked against the 9-10 cell and an 11 or 12 row against the 11-12 cell, and the
  progression says how grade 9 differs from 10 and 11 from 12, since the continuum does not. The four band names the
  pages use (K to 2, 3 to 5, 6 to 8, 9 to 12) match the files, K to 2 being the file's PK to 2 without PK.
- **Read in full:** the grades 3 to 5 band file 2026-11-16; the PK to 2, 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 files 2026-11-21, from
  copies kept outside the repo (S:\CurioClass-sources\library\aasl-2026-11-21, read once, ids and figures only).
  Example of the grain, in our own words: grade 3 uses a teacher-given source for background and simple
  note-making; grade 4 picks an interesting part of a broad topic and compares primary and secondary sources the
  teacher chose; grade 5 judges which questions facts can answer, weighs print, video and online sources for
  relevance and builds a bibliography to a model. In K to 2 the bold skills include finding picture books by their
  labels and shelf order, looking a book up in the catalog by author, title or key word, naming who made a site,
  and crediting authors; in 6 to 8, citing every source in a set style and deciding what fair use allows; in 9 to
  12, reading laterally for background, search filters and operators, and copyright, open licenses and asking a
  maker's permission. The per-grade priority skills, in our words, are the rows of data/progressions/library.csv;
  where a row places a continuum bold skill in a different grade, its note says why.
- **Terms:** no license or copyright line on the guide pages or in the band file. Unclear, so reference only: used to
  place skills in grades and to check our list, never copied.
- **Use (recommended, ours):** each course's progression names priority skills per grade in our own words, tagged
  with the AASL ids they serve, and checked against the continuum's bold skills for that grade. Where the two differ,
  the progression says why.

## 3. How the standard maps to our courses

Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): one course per grade K to 5, then one course for
grades 6 to 8 and one for grades 9 to 12, eight courses. The K to 5 courses each plan one year, so the skills grow
every year. The 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 courses spread across their grades, about one session every two weeks, taught
alongside other classes so research skills come back each year. Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-followups-1).
Course ids: `library-k`, `library-1` to `library-5`, `library-6-8`, `library-9-12`, one row each in data/courses.csv
in the band-split form of health, PE and arts (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1).

**Ownership** (ours, untested). Each of the 68 ids is owned by exactly one grade, the school year where it is first
met at the depth a check can score on its own (not the "with help" level); every other year where a course returns
to it carries `~ID` with a one-line note on how that year differs. Grades 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 get a progression row
per school year (6, 7, 8; 9, 10, 11, 12) even though each is one course: the progression's grades are the school
years, the course is how they are taught. Each row's `assumes` names only the earlier library row it builds on
(empty in kindergarten; each school year's row assumes the library row of the year before, so no grade is skipped),
since the gate accepts only rows of the same file there; an ELA or social studies row it builds on goes in
`cross_subject` (section 5). The gate's library entry in scripts/progression.py uses the standards file, the
anchor, ids with no grade (any grade may own one; the one-owner check does the work) and
data/progressions/library-excluded.csv for the ids section 4 places elsewhere. Settled (Cory, 2026-11-21): own once, at
the school year where the id is first checked alone; `library-6-8` stays one course across grades 6 to 8, with a
row per school year.

| Courses | AASL | Priority skill emphasis (ours; set grade by grade in data/progressions/library.csv) |
|---|---|---|
| K, 1 and 2 (one course each) | All six foundations, mostly at the "with help" level (carried as `~`, owned only where the grade checks an id alone: Ownership, above) | Asking a question, finding a book by its label and shelf order, retelling what a source says, saying who made it and where it came from, sharing with the class (checked against the PK to 2 band file, 2026-11-21) |
| 3, 4 and 5 (one course each) | All six | Key words, searching a catalog and a chosen site, note-making, primary and secondary sources, a simple bibliography, first lateral checks of a site |
| Grades 6 to 8 (one course) | All six | Search strategy, lateral reading, judging evidence and who is behind it, paraphrase and quotation, citation in one style, fair use basics, checking text made by a machine like any other source (who made it, what it cites) |
| Grades 9 to 12 (one course) | All six | Research question design, databases, search operators and filters, and open sources, lateral reading on hard cases, synthesis across sources, full citation, copyright, licenses, fair use and asking a maker's permission (checked against the 9 to 12 band file, 2026-11-21), checking and disclosing the use of machine-made text, sharing a research product |

**Partial bases for 6 to 12.** Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): the CC BY
online-reasoning lessons and the open web-literacy book named in docs/reviews/library-open-material-2026-11-16.md
are partial bases for the 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 courses. Only items under an open license are adapted, in our voice,
with the source in the day's credit, and each item's license is re-read at source when it is first adapted (the
scout's reading of 2026-11-16 is the only one on file); no company or product name appears in student, teacher or family copy.

Every unit names host tasks from the grade's other courses (bar criterion 4): research for a science investigation, a
social studies source question, an ELA research piece. Each also carries a stand-alone host task (APPROACH.md).

## 4. What is left out, and why

- The AASL school librarian and school library frameworks: they describe adults and programs, not learners.
- Reading promotion and literature appreciation (the continuum's multiple literacies standard) beyond what a research
  unit needs: ELA owns reading; library units may use the same books.
- The continuum's design thinking strand: science and computer science own the design cycle; library leans on them
  and keeps only self-chosen making inside a research product (V.B.2, below).
- **By id.** The gate and bar criterion C1 work on ids, not themes. Each of the 68 is either excluded with a reason in
  `data/progressions/library-excluded.csv`, or owned once by a library row and carried as `~` in other years
  (section 3, Ownership); the owner row meets it through a research task (for example II.B.2, weighing
  perspectives, on the day two sources disagree; III.C.1, feedback, on the revise day of a research product).
  Carrying an id as `~` never stands in for its one owner row. Everything not placed below is met through a
  research task (Cory, 2026-11-21).
- **The eight ids checked against the second edition chart** (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1: an id that
  belongs elsewhere goes to its receiving subject and is never dropped). Three stay in library, owned by a library
  row: III.A.1 (choosing to widen and deepen a question by researching with others: a group inquiry day), V.C.2
  (working out with others a new way to find something out, such as a class survey or an observation plan: an
  inquiry day) and V.B.2 (keeping going on a self-chosen making product through trial and error: the made product of
  a research unit). Five are placed in a receiving subject, listed in library-excluded.csv with that subject as the
  reason, and named there by the receiving subject's own page:

| Id | Receiving subject and course band | Why there |
|---|---|---|
| V.B.1 | Science, K to 12: the engineering design rows and the `design-cycle` model (from `sk-define-problem`, K-2-ETS1-1) | The design, test and improve cycle is engineering design, which science teaches every year; computer science uses the same loop |
| V.A.1 | ELA, K to 12: the range of reading and writing rows (RL and RI .10, W.10: `ek-group-reading`, `e3-reading-range` onward, `e3-writing-tasks` onward) | Reading widely in many forms and writing for many purposes is ELA's own range standard |
| V.D.1 | SEL, all four bands (`sel-k-2` to `sel-9-12`): SM3, keeping on task and pushing yourself | Trying again when a task gets hard is self-management, taught on everyday cases |
| V.D.2 | SEL, all four bands: SA8, skill grows with effort, practice and help | The growth view of skill is a self-awareness sub-skill |
| III.D.2 | SEL, all four bands: RS4, working as a team | Treating the group's learning as one's own job is a teamwork skill, taught where SEL teaches teams |

- **What a school may swap or leave out** (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1; the same rule as SEL, PE and
  arts, docs/courses/arts/STANDARD.md section 5): a school or family may swap a case or leave out a unit for its
  community's reasons. Swapping a case for another keeps the unit's ids met; leaving out a unit means the course page
  and the unit's family note name the ids not claimed that year. K to 5 case sets stay neutral: no politics,
  elections, religion, identity groups or conflict (APPROACH.md, "Sensitive ground", item 1).

## 5. Cross-subject leans

**Who teaches what first.** ELA owns the research cycle and the writing of it (the model `research-cycle`; rows
`ek-shared-research` and `e1-shared-research` to `e12-research`), the list of sources in research writing (grade 4,
`e4-research`, which builds on library's grade 2 credit line and grade 3 source list), finding answers quickly across
several print and digital sources (grade 5, `e5-locate-sources`), source credibility in writing (grade 6, `e6-short-research`)
and a standard citation format (grade 7, `e7-research-questions`). Social studies owns sourcing and corroboration
(the models `source-sourcing` and `corroboration`, from the grade 2 rows `ss2-source-maker` and `ss2-two-accounts`)
and news reading (`ss8-news-literacy`, grade 8;
`us1-news-reading`, grade 11). Library owns what neither does: searching as a skill (changing a query, catalogs
and databases, choosing among results), leaving the page to check who is behind it (lateral reading, the first time
it is taught as a method: owned by a library row before grade 8, where `ss8-news-literacy` uses it on news), going upstream to the first source, honest use of others' work across media (not only in
writing), and choosing among tools. Where a library row meets an ELA or social studies skill it names that row's id in
`cross_subject` (for example `ela:e5-locate-sources`; never a model name, and never in `assumes`, section 3); the
progression says which came first in each grade. Re-read against ELA grades 3 to 7 on 2026-11-21
(docs/reviews/progression-library-2026-11-21.md): no conflict in the emphasis cells; two ownership lines above were
corrected and grade 3 note categories moved to grade 4.

- **ELA:** the research writing standards W.7 and W.8 from kindergarten and W.9 from grade 4, and informational
  reading.
- **Social studies:** C3 dimension 3 (sources and evidence) and Massachusetts news and media literacy (NML), from
  grade 8 (`ss8-news-literacy`). Library owns the lateral reading method; social studies uses it on its sources.
- **Science:** science rows obtain and combine information from texts, maps and media (for example `s2-water-where`,
  `s3-climates`, `s4-energy-resources`, `s5-protect-resources`; `s8-human-trait-technology` asks for reliable
  sources), but none teaches judging a source as a method. Library names such rows as host tasks and teaches the
  judging.
- **Computer science:** computer science owns how machine-made text tools work, in its own rows; library owns
  checking what they produce, and assumes no computer science row.
# Library and research skills: approach (layer 3)

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-21 (Desk q-prework-open-2). Written 2026-11-16, read cold and fixed 2026-11-20 (docs/reviews/prework-library-read-2026-11-20.md).** Short by design: it fills the subject
slots of the unit and section plan (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md) and leans on the shared approach (docs/APPROACH.md),
the lens (docs/agents/lenses/library.md) and the standard (STANDARD.md). Ours, untested. K to 5 courses meet one or two
sessions a week all year (Cory, 2026-11-15); the 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 courses about one session every two weeks
across their grades (Cory, 2026-11-16); the frame says which. Fixed again 2026-11-21 from the second read and
calibration (docs/reviews/prework-library-fixes-2026-11-20.md). Fields not named on this page (unit field 1,
section field 2) and the shared parts of the named ones are filled as UNIT-PLAN.md says, with nothing added for
library.

## The kinds of day, in library's words

| Shared kind | In library | What it looks like |
|---|---|---|
| Understanding | Show-me day | A skill modeled on the host task: the teacher thinks aloud through a search, a lateral check or a citation |
| Fluency | Practice day | Short, varied tries of the skill with feedback: three claims checked laterally, five sources cited |
| Application | Inquiry day | Students run part of the cycle on the host task themselves: question, search, judge, note, make, share |
| Check | Assessment day | A search, judgment or citation actually done on a new case, scored on a short rubric, plus a few items |
| Respond | Respond day | The product or notes revised from feedback |

A unit runs at least one full inquiry cycle on its host task, with the product shared. Each unit names the kind of
host task and one example from the grade's core courses (by progression row id), for the teacher to schedule
against, and also carries a stand-alone host task of its own (an invented question with a closed source set), so the
unit can run when no core task is in step, or at home where a family uses no other Curio Class course. The scope and
sequence records both.

## The form of daily evidence

- **Show-me and practice days:** an exit ticket that asks the student to do the skill once on a new case (check a
  claim and say what other sources said; fix a citation), with error letters for the usual slips.
- **Inquiry days:** the working notes (question, search terms tried, sources with a judgment line each).
- **K to 2:** the teacher's observation of one named skill per child, on a class checklist kept by the teacher and
  thrown away at the end of the unit, plus a drawing or a spoken answer.
- **Once a unit, a self-check** (the cycle's reflect step; bar C8): after the product is shared, the student rates
  their own use of the skill card's steps and names one thing to do differently next time (K to 2: pointing at
  pictured steps and saying it aloud).

## What library adds to the plan fields

- Unit field 2: the host task and its course (and the stand-alone host task, above); the AASL ids the unit owns,
  each with its phrase from data/standards/aasl-words.txt, never the competency text (STANDARD.md section 1), and
  the grade's priority skills in our own words; the product and its audience; the day the skill card is withdrawn.
- Unit field 3 (where it sits): builds on (the earlier library row by id, and any ELA or social studies row it
  leans on, from its `cross_subject`); leads to; comes back in.
- Unit field 4 (readiness): one short item on the skill the unit builds on (find the maker of a page; name a
  source's kind); the ten-minute reteach is the earlier unit's show-me case.
- Unit field 5 (end-of-unit assessment): a search, a judgment or a citation actually done on a new case, scored on
  a short rubric, plus a few items. Never a quiz on the names of the steps alone.
- Unit field 6 (error register): the usual slips (trusting the look of a page, staying on the page to judge it,
  copying instead of noting, a citation missing its date or maker).
- Unit field 7 (response allocation): the rubric is teacher-judged, with no hands-up self-marking, and the respond
  day's groups come from the class record sheet by the rubric step missed; items with one answer are checked on the
  page.
- Unit field 8 (tools and supplies): printed source sets, invented pages and search results, the research notebook;
  no product or service names.
- Unit field 9 (family note): in English and Spanish: the skill's name and steps and one thing to try together at
  home (checking who is behind a page you both use); if a school leaves the unit out, the ids not claimed that
  year (STANDARD.md section 4).
- Unit field 10 (sources): what was read and how much, what is from memory, no claim of effect; the record of whose
  work and voices the unit's source set shows (C12; Sensitive ground, item 5); a line pointing to the course-level
  logic model (C16).
- Section field 1: before you teach: the AASL ids and the priority skill, the skill as an adult uses it, and why the
  method beats a checklist of a page's looks. What the teacher page must explain: the sensitive ground the section
  touches (below); on a day with open search, the stop rule and what the adult does if an unsafe result appears; and,
  if a student tells you something or a result shows harm to a child, health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md,
  "Standards and the teacher page").
- Section field 3: each day's evidence in the form above.
- Section field 4: expected slips beside a sound example.
- Section field 5 (what is carried, and instruments): the unit's skill card (the steps of the one search, check or
  citation move the unit teaches). The teacher holds it on the first show-me day, thinking aloud on an invented
  case; pairs hold it on the first practice day; it is withdrawn on the first inquiry day, which the section plan
  names. The unit check is taken without it. A skill returns in later units with the card for one day, then without
  it. K to 2 use the same steps as three or four pictured words said aloud. A room with nothing: the print path
  (printed source sets, invented pages and search results), with no library access assumed.
- Section field 6: the kind of day in library's words; a day opens with one earlier skill tried on a short case or
  a source prompt, five minutes at most; a new skill is shown on a show-me day before students try it (rule 13).
- Section field 7 (talk and writing): a search-aloud routine (one student types or points, one says what they see,
  one records), and sentence frames for a judgment ("I trust this less because...").
- Section field 8 (practice that returns): one earlier skill, named, tried once on a new case.
- Section field 9 (supports): English learners (C11): the unit's six to eight words (source, claim, search, cite and
  the like, whose everyday meanings differ) taught with a picture and a frame, and a key line that says a sound
  judgment given in the home language or by pointing is a language gap, a wrong move is a content gap; access: the
  print path and read-aloud pages; check accommodations: the exit ticket read aloud, answered orally, by pointing on
  the printed set or with a scribe; reading load: source texts at the grade's level or read aloud, with a shorter
  source set for students who read below it; extension: the same skill on a harder case, never the next grade's.
- Section field 10 (two rooms): a parent at a table with one device or none, using the printed set (and the stop
  rule on a device); one projector: the teacher shows the invented page or search results and pairs record their
  checks on paper.
- Section field 11 (not in this section): what is left to another section, course or subject.

## Sites, search and real cases

Practice and check pages use invented sites, invented search results and described captures, with kinds named
generically ("an online encyclopedia", "a fact-checking site", "a video site"). Inquiry days may use the real web
under the teacher's guidance and the stop rule below; the student page then says "your search results" and names no
site. A teacher page may say "swap in a current case" without naming a product. No real site is named in any
page (Cory, 2026-11-21).

## What a tool may and may not do

A tool may give practice on an invented set of pages, searches and sources (a closed set, so every result is the
same for every student), check a citation against a model, sort claims into "checked" and "not yet checked", and keep
a private research notebook on the device. It may not use the live web or a real search service, record what a
student searches for or types, give a verdict on a real site, or write the student's notes or product.

## Sensitive ground, and the read each needs

Each read is a fresh-agent read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md, kind `sensitivity`), on the unit's case set.

1. **Cases about politics, health, elections, religion, identity groups or conflict.** K to 5 case sets stay neutral:
   no politics, elections, religion, identity groups or conflict (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1). In 6 to
   12, cases are invented or chosen evenly across views; hoax or hate pages are described or shown in an invented
   form, never reproduced. A school may swap a case or leave out a unit by the SEL, PE and arts rule (STANDARD.md
   section 4: a swapped case keeps its ids met; a left-out unit's ids are named as not claimed that year on the
   course page and in the family note). Read: every unit's case set, K to 12.
2. **Unsafe or upsetting search results** (adult content, violence, scams). Students use a closed set or a
   teacher-checked list; no child is sent into open search without the stop rule (close the page, tell the adult, no
   blame), and the teacher page and the home note say what the adult does if one appears (section fields 1 and 10).
   Read: the stop rule, the K to 5 plans and every 6 to 12 plan with an open-search inquiry day.
3. **Privacy.** No student types a name, school, place or photo into a search or a site; a tool stores nothing off
   the device. Read: the tools reader's privacy check.
4. **Honest use taught without shaming.** A student who copied is helped to cite, never accused in front of the
   class. Read: part of the read of every unit that teaches honest use, in every band.
5. **Whose voices a source set shows** (C12): each unit records whose work and voices appear. Read: the same reads.
6. **Copyright and fair use** as general guidance, never legal advice; no claim that a use is lawful. Read: kind
   `rights`, on the 6 to 12 copyright, license and fair use days.
7. **Machine-made text and images as a source:** no claim that any tool is reliable or unreliable; the check is the
   same as for any source (who made it, what it cites). Read: the 6 to 12 plans.
8. **Asking people: interviews and surveys.** Questions stay on a neutral class topic and are checked by the teacher
   before use; no one is asked about family history, home, beliefs, identity, health, money, food or meals, a family
   member's job, or immigration or other status unless they raise it, and that is not recorded. Any trusted adult or
   a school staff member may stand in for a family member, and a first-person source set may stand in for any
   interview. Interviews outside school are arranged through the school or a family, in a public or supervised
   setting, with the person's consent (and a parent's, for anyone under 18), recorded only with consent, and reported
   without a full name. No classmate is asked to speak for a group. Surveys are of the class, on neutral things, with
   no names recorded. Read: every unit with an interview or survey.

## Pacing and dose

Settled (Cory, 2026-11-15 and 2026-11-16): K to 5 meet one or two sessions a week; 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 about one
session every two weeks across their grades. Default time, ours and untested: K to 2, 30 sessions a year of 25
minutes (about 12.5 hours); 3 to 5, 30 sessions of 35 minutes (about 17.5 hours), both at the one-a-week end of the
settled range; 6 to 8 and 9 to 12, 18 sessions a year of 45 minutes (about 13.5 hours a year: 54 sessions, about
40.5 hours, across grades 6 to 8; 72 sessions, about 54 hours, across grades 9 to 12). Cory kept these (2026-11-21).
A frame may change them with a reason, and each course's scope and sequence names its short path (bar C2). No national or
professional body publishes a time figure for library instruction that we found (the AASL scheduling position
statement, revised 2019, sets none; searched 2026-11-20). The one time figure behind a method here is the 2022
lateral reading field study (about 500 high school students, six 50-minute lessons, from the Stanford news release;
the article was not re-read), so the 9 to 12 course holds at least that much lateral reading and claims no more.

## Evidence and sources

What rests on what: the AASL framework (both editions read for ids, 2026-11-20; the second edition chart read
again for our own words, 2026-11-21); all four of the continuum's band files read in full (3 to 5 on 2026-11-16,
the rest on 2026-11-21; STANDARD.md section 2); the open lessons named in the base-text scout
(docs/reviews/library-open-material-2026-11-16.md). No claim of effect is made for any course, and none has been
tried with children; each course has a one-page logic model on its course page (C16).

## Settled questions

- Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): one course per grade K to 5, one for 6 to 8, one
  for 9 to 12; the online-reasoning lessons and the web-literacy book are partial bases for 6 to 12 (STANDARD.md
  section 3). Sessions a week were settled 2026-11-15 (above).
- Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-followups-1): the 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 courses spread across their grades, about one session every two weeks.
# Lens: library and research skills

Subject pre-work, 2026-11-16, in the shape of the core lenses (P5a, docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md). The expert reads this
before any library frame, scope and sequence, unit plan or day. It is a way of seeing, not a bar: the bar is part 11
of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md (ours, untested). The standard is in docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md: AASL second
edition, with the Empire State continuum as the grade check set.

## What good teaching of library and research skills looks like

**An inquiry cycle every year, at growing depth.** Ask, find, judge, use, share, reflect (the continuum's connect,
wonder, investigate, construct, express, reflect). Young students do the whole cycle with a lot of help on a small
question; older students do it alone on a hard one.

**Skills are taught on real work.** Every unit hosts a task from another course the same grade is doing (a science
question, a social studies source, an ELA research piece). Research skills taught on nothing are forgotten.

**Judging a source means leaving it.** Students learn to open new tabs and ask what others say about a site or claim
before reading it closely (lateral reading), to find the original (go upstream), and to skip the first result when
it is an ad (click restraint). A checklist run on the page itself is the old method and fails on polished fakes.

**Questions drive searching.** Students turn a topic into questions, pick key words, try, look at what came back and
change the search. Library catalogues, databases, reference works and the open web are each taught for what they are
good at.

**Honest use of others' work, every band.** Saying where an idea came from (K to 2), a simple list of sources (3 to 5),
paraphrase, quotation, one citation style and fair use basics (6 to 8), copyright, open licences, fair use in depth,
asking a maker's permission and AI-made text (9 to 12); bands checked against the continuum's band files
(Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1).

**Making and sharing.** Research ends in a product for a real audience, and students judge their own process.

## Errors reviewers catch

**Progression and frame**
- A one-off orientation or "library skills week" instead of a year of spaced units (bar fails if).
- A grade with no named priority skills, or a skill owned twice at the same depth, or a skill the continuum places
  two or more grades later taught with no reason.
- Lateral reading owned by both library and social studies with no line saying who teaches it first.

**Scope and sequence and unit**
- No host task from another course named.
- Source judging taught as a website checklist (domain endings, a nice design, an about page) without lateral moves.
- A citation unit with no research to cite.
- The end-of-unit assessment is recall of terms, with no search, judgement or citation actually done.
- AASL competency text copied, or a claim of AASL alignment.

**Day**
- A live web page relied on with no dated capture or teacher swap note.
- Students sent to a paid database or a library they may not have, with no free path (no library access is assumed;
  Cory, 2026-11-11).
- A product, platform or search company named on a student page.
- A claim that the method raises scores in general (one field study supports lateral reading in high school only).

## What this lens does not cover

The scored bar. Reading for pleasure and literature (ELA). Id checks (scripts/standards.py). Whether a source may be
adapted (BASE-TEXTS.md and the scout).

## Sources, read into our own words

- AASL, National School Library Standards, second edition, learner framework (2025; structure confirmed 2026-11-16;
  the chart read 2026-11-21 for our own words; competency text not quoted).
- Stripling, Empire State Information Fluency Continuum (2019; the 3 to 5 band read 2026-11-16, the PK to 2, 6 to 8 and
  9 to 12 bands 2026-11-21).
- Digital Inquiry Group, Civic Online Reasoning (licence page and lesson list read 2026-11-16; lessons not read).
- Wineburg and McGrew on lateral reading by professional fact-checkers; Wineburg and others, the 2022 district field
  study in high school government classes (from memory, not re-read).
- Caulfield, Web Literacy for Student Fact-Checkers, 2017 (contents read 2026-11-16).
- Kuhlthau, Guided Inquiry and the information search process (from memory).
