## Seams

### Picked up from Library and Research Skills, kindergarten

Every grade 1 row assumes one kindergarten row, used the year before, so each opens as a five-minute retrieval task
inside a day (lesson 16), never a readiness check. Day 1 of every unit stays clean for the hook and the question.

| Kindergarten row (last use) | Unit here | Grade 1 row that picks it up | Treatment |
|---|---|---|---|
| k-wonder: an "I wonder" question of one's own (kindergarten unit 1) | 1 | g1-what-i-know | Retrieval, unit 1 day 2: one "I wonder" about the day's picture, before the know and wonder sheet |
| k-class-inquiry: the whole cycle as four pictures, research-cycle (kindergarten unit 1, run in all four units) | 1 | g1-class-book; g1-star-and-wish | Retrieval, unit 1 day 2: the four cycle pictures put in order; the card goes back on the wall |
| k-picture-book-labels: a book fetched from a named bin, story books and fact books sorted (kindergarten unit 2) | 1 | g1-story-or-fact-book | Retrieval, unit 1 day 3: fetch the fact-book bin by its label |
| k-listen-and-retell: listening for the part that answers a held question (kindergarten unit 3) | 2 | g1-find-in-a-book | Retrieval, unit 2 day 2: hold a question, hand up at the answering sentence |
| k-library-visit: book care, borrowing day, the stop rule as three pictured words (kindergarten unit 2) | 2 | g1-device-stop-rule | Retrieval, unit 2 day 3: point at the three stop pictures in order |
| k-talk-in-group: turns in a group of three, saying what each friend added (kindergarten unit 4) | 3 | g1-group-helpers | Retrieval, unit 3 day 2: one group turn with the talking object |
| k-who-made-book: the author and illustrator named on the cover (kindergarten unit 4) | 3 | g1-author-and-title | Retrieval, unit 3 day 3: point to the maker's name on a cover card |

Every kindergarten row's hands_on names a grade 1 row, and each grade 1 row's assumes names it back. The kindergarten
year-end note to this teacher is class-level only (the checklist rule), so the retrievals, not records, show who
needs the move again.

### Handed on to Library and Research Skills, grade 2

| This course hands on | Unit here | Grade 2 row that picks it up | What grade 2 does with it |
|---|---|---|---|
| g1-what-i-know: the know and wonder sheet; AASL.I.A.2 owned | 1 | g2-question-to-research | Says what is known, then turns a wonder into a question-word question a book could answer |
| g1-story-or-fact-book: the information book chosen for a question, and why | 1 | g2-kinds-of-sources | More kinds of source matched to a question; owns AASL.IV.A.2 |
| g1-find-in-a-book: cover, contents, headings, pictures and labels to find the answering page | 2 | g2-catalog-lookup; g2-fact-sort; g2-two-books-differ | The book found by catalog and shelf order; found facts sorted into boxes; two books compared |
| g1-device-stop-rule: only the page the teacher opened, the stop rule, never typing a name or school | 2 | g2-catalog-lookup | The stop rule on any device day of the catalog unit |
| g1-author-and-title: "I found this in the book (title) by (author)", copied with help | 3 | g2-who-made-it; g2-credit-line | Who made a book or page and why; a credit line written alone, owning AASL.VI.B.2 |
| g1-group-helpers: pictured jobs (finder, holder, teller), swapped, what each helper added | 3 | g2-group-research | A group plans two questions to ask a person and names what each member added |
| g1-class-book: a class information book, one page per child (research-cycle) | 4 | g2-research-share | A pair poster with two found facts, a picture and a credit line |
| g1-star-and-wish: one star and one wish on a classmate's page | 4 | g2-research-share | Saying back what another pair's poster taught them, and the spoken look-back |

Every grade 1 row hands on to a grade 2 row, and each grade 2 row's assumes names it back.

## Models carried

| Model | Where it comes from | Introduced here | Used again here |
|---|---|---|---|
| research-cycle | Library kindergarten (k-class-inquiry, model_out), four pictured steps: ask, find, share, reflect; g1-class-book carries it (model_in and model_out) | Not new: unit 1 day 2 retrieves the four pictures in order and runs the cycle once on the host question | Units 2, 3 and 4 each run the whole cycle once; unit 4's class information book is its first written product, one page per child |

The model goes on to grade 2 (g2-research-share, model_in and model_out), which adds the "check" picture.

## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Inquiry phases | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | What we know, and which book. *What do we already know about a topic, and which book could tell us more?* | 6 | g1-what-i-know; g1-story-or-fact-book | AASL.I.A.2 | Connect, wonder, investigate (choosing a source), express, reflect | Before a read-aloud or a picture set, drawing or saying one thing already known about the topic and one wonder, on a two-box know and wonder sheet, and afterwards marking any "know" that turned out not quite right (AASL.I.A.2, owned here: "Say what you already know about a topic before you start looking," checked alone: a real prior fact, unprompted). Review: the "I wonder" question (AASL.V.C.1, owned in kindergarten by k-wonder). Given a question, choosing between a story book and an information book and saying why the information book is more likely to answer it, then fetching the information-book bin by its label. Begun here with help, owned later: listing the kinds of sources likely to hold an answer (AASL.IV.A.2, owned by g2-kinds-of-sources, grade 2). Out: turning a wonder into a question a book could answer (grade 2, g2-question-to-research); kinds of source beyond story and information books (grade 2). Days: show-me (hook: a sunflower taller than the teacher in a picture, the know and wonder sheet modeled on it), practice (opening retrieval: the four cycle pictures in order and one "I wonder" (k-class-inquiry, k-wonder); sheets on two new pictures), show-me (opening retrieval: fetch the fact-book bin by its label (k-picture-book-labels); story book or information book for a question, and why), inquiry (the cycle on the host or stand-alone question: sheet, choose the information book, the teacher reads, each child checks a "know" and draws one found fact; share circle), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the self-check and a new wonder for the board, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "think, draw what I know, draw what I wonder, which book?" pictured; withdrawn on the inquiry day. End-of-unit evidence (about 17 minutes for 30): a sheet item, the teacher showing a new topic picture (a snail) and reading the prompts aloud: each child draws or writes one fact they already know and one wonder (g1-what-i-know, about 8 minutes, marked from the page: a real prior fact); then a scanned pointing item: each child holds a strip with a story cover and an information cover, the teacher names a question and each points at the book likely to answer it (about 2 minutes); then a whisper walk: each child says why (g1-story-or-fact-book, about 15 seconds a child, about 7 minutes). Tools: the know and wonder sheet, the cover strips, the sunflower read-aloud and a short sunflower story, the snail picture (Curio Class writes all of them). Host: what the class already knows about shadows and the sky, science grade 1 unit 1 (days 1 to 8), or about how a tree stays standing, science grade 1 unit 2 (days 10 to 21); stand-alone: "How tall do sunflowers grow?" | First because every later step needs it: a child who says what they know and picks the right kind of book can then look inside it (unit 2). The know and wonder sheet is the connect step, the first of the cycle, and grade 1's one owned id; it also opens the class information book in unit 4. Placed in the first weeks, beside science's first two units, so the class has a real topic to know and wonder about | Candidate: the continuum's grade 1 cells on telling easy from harder questions (a quick sort of the board's wonders on the practice day) and on stating the "big picture" idea of a read-aloud orally or in a drawing, at the share step | Neutral topics only (sunflowers, snails, the sky). A "know" that turns out not quite right is praised as good checking, never corrected in front of the class; no child is asked what they know from home. A child who prefers not to speak may point or whisper. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its read-alouds and pictures (C12) |
| 2 | Finding the page. *Where in a book, or on the page our teacher opened, is the answer to our question?* | 6 | g1-find-in-a-book; g1-device-stop-rule | None | Investigate (navigation within a source; safe use), express, reflect | Using the cover, the contents page, headings, pictures and labels to find the page in an information book that answers a class question, then showing the page to a partner: searching inside one source instead of reading from the start. On the shared class device (or the printed card frame), using only the page the teacher opened, the stop rule on pretend cases (a pop-up, a picture that feels wrong: close it, tell the adult, no blame), and never typing one's name or school. Begun here with help, owned later: searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, owned by g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4); using information, devices and media responsibly (AASL.VI.A.1, owned by g3-safe-searching, grade 3). Out: the index and glossary (ELA grade 1 and 2 text features, and grade 2 review here); the catalog and shelf order (grade 2, g2-catalog-lookup); any open search (not used in grade 1). Days: show-me (hook: a big information book and the question "what do tree roots do?", the teacher thinks aloud with contents and headings), practice (opening retrieval: hold a question, hand up at the answering sentence (k-listen-and-retell); pairs find the answering page in three short mini-books and show a partner), show-me (opening retrieval: the three stop pictures in order (k-library-visit); the class screen: only the teacher's page, the stop rule on pretend cases, never typing a name or school), inquiry (the cycle on the host or stand-alone question: find the page in a book and on the teacher's page, draw and label the fact, partner share, self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the partner shares, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "look at the contents, find the heading, turn to the page, show it" pictured, beside the three stop pictures. End-of-unit evidence (about 16 minutes for 30): a sheet item: a printed contents page and a two-page spread from a mini-book not seen before, a question read aloud, and each child circles the heading and the page number that answer it (g1-find-in-a-book, about 7 minutes); then a scanned pointing item: as the teacher describes two pretend cases (a pop-up asking for a name; a picture that feels wrong), each child points at the stop steps in order (about 4 minutes); then a whisper walk: "what do we never type?" (g1-device-stop-rule, about 10 seconds a child, about 5 minutes). Tools: three mini-books, contents sheets, pretend screen cards and a card frame (Curio Class writes them), the shared class device. Host: how a plant's parts help it, s1-parts-mimic in science grade 1 unit 2 (days 10 to 21); stand-alone: "What do tree roots do?" | After unit 1 because the child now has the right kind of book in hand and needs to get the answer out of it; the device move joins it because a teacher-opened page is the second place grade 1 looks for an answer, and the stop rule must be in place before any device day. Before unit 3, whose teams find facts in a mini-book by its headings | Candidate: the continuum's grade 1 cells on interpreting information in pictures and simple charts with help (a labeled picture on the practice day) and on knowing what the internet is, said in one sentence on the device show-me day | The stop rule: the teacher page says what the adult does if an upsetting picture ever appears (close it, the child tells, no blame) and points to health's steps if a child tells something worrying (APPROACH.md, Section field 1). No child types anything; pretend cases never show a real site, and the pretend pop-up asks for a name so the class can practice not giving it. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its mini-books (C12) |
| 3 | Finding it together. *How can a team find a fact, and say which book it came from?* | 6 | g1-group-helpers; g1-author-and-title | None | Investigate (with others), express (crediting), reflect | Groups of three take pictured jobs (finder, holder, teller) to find one fact in a picture set or mini-book, swap jobs the next time, and at the end name what each helper added. When a fact is shared, saying where it came from: "I found this in the book (title) by (author)," and copying the title and author from the cover onto a sticky note, with help; a found fact came from a maker (review of k-who-made-book). Begun here with help, owned later: speaking up with findings in a research group and naming what each classmate adds (AASL.III.D.1 and AASL.II.A.1, owned by g3-research-team, grade 3); naming who made each work (AASL.VI.B.2, owned by g2-credit-line, grade 2). Out: a credit line written alone (grade 2); asking people questions (grade 2, g2-group-research). Days: show-me (hook and the three job cards), show-me (opening retrieval: one group turn with the talking object (k-talk-in-group); the "I found this in..." frame and copying title and author onto a sticky note), practice (opening retrieval: point to the maker's name on a cover card (k-who-made-book); groups take jobs on a new picture set and swap; the teller uses the frame), inquiry (the cycle on the host or stand-alone question: each group finds a fact, the teller tells it with title and author, the sticky note goes on the group's drawing, the group names what each helper added; self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with a share of the groups' drawings, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "finder, holder, teller; I found this in... by..." pictured. End-of-unit evidence (about 18 minutes for 30): a sheet item: each child copies the title and author from a new cover card onto a sticky note (g1-author-and-title, about 4 minutes, marked from the note: both copied so a reader can find the book; spelling help allowed); then a station of three at the check table with a new picture set while the others sort cover cards into story and information books in the book corner (practiced in unit 1): the children take jobs, find a fact, and each names what one helper added (g1-group-helpers, about 1.4 minutes a group, ten groups, about 14 minutes). Tools: job cards, cover cards, sticky notes, the bird read-aloud and two picture sets (Curio Class writes the read-aloud, sets and cards). Host: a bird's body parts, ela grade 1 unit 3 (days 79 to 119); stand-alone: "How do birds build their nests?" | After unit 2 because a team needs the finding-inside-a-book move before it shares the work of finding; the source line joins it because a teller now passes on a fact that is not their own idea, and grade 2's credit line needs title and author copied first. Before unit 4, whose class book pages each carry a source line | Candidate: the continuum's grade 1 cells on participating in discussion to draw a conclusion (the group says what its facts add up to at the share) and on knowing it is wrong to copy from an author or a peer, said aloud with the source line | Honest use without shaming (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 4): a child who calls a found fact "mine" is helped to say "I found it in...", never corrected in front of the group. Groups are mixed by the teacher, never by background; every child holds every job across the unit. A child who prefers not to speak may be finder or holder that day. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its books and pictures (C12) |
| 4 | Our class information book. *What can our class find out together, and how do we make each page better?* | 7 | g1-class-book; g1-star-and-wish | None | Connect, investigate, construct, express (product and feedback), reflect | The class runs the research cycle on one question and makes a class information book, one page per child: a labeled drawing and a sentence of one found fact that answers part of the question, with a source line ("I found this in... by..."). Children give a classmate one star (something the page does well, such as a clear label) and one wish (something to add) with a pictured frame; the maker chooses whether to use the wish and revises the page. The book is read aloud and each child says one thing a friend's page taught them. Review: the know and wonder sheet (unit 1), finding the page (unit 2), the source line (unit 3). Begun here with help, owned later: making a product that shows what the research taught (AASL.I.B.3, owned by g3-make-product, grade 3); responding to a classmate's work and giving specific, kind advice (AASL.I.C.1 and AASL.I.C.2, owned by g3-gallery-feedback, grade 3). Out: a pair product and the spoken look-back on the whole cycle (grade 2, g2-research-share); written feedback notes (grade 3). Days: show-me (hook: a finished class information book from a pretend class; the page template), inquiry (opening retrieval: a know and wonder sheet on the class topic (g1-what-i-know, unit 1); each child picks one wonder and finds the answering page), inquiry (opening retrieval: the source line (g1-author-and-title, unit 3); each child drafts a page with a sticky-note source line), show-me (star and wish modeled on a pretend page: specific and kind, not "I like it"), practice (stars and wishes in pairs on classmates' drafts; each maker revises the page), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the class book read aloud and each child saying one thing a friend's page taught them, about ten minutes, then the self-check and the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "find one fact, draw it, label it, say where it came from; one star, one wish" pictured. End-of-unit evidence (about 17 minutes for 30): a sheet item: the teacher reads a short passage not heard before twice with a held question, and each child draws and labels one fact that answers it and writes or dictates one sentence (g1-class-book on a new case, about 10 minutes, marked from the page: the fact answers the question); then each child gets a pretend classmate's page, draws a star by one thing done well (sheet item, about 2 minutes) and says one wish in a whisper walk (g1-star-and-wish, about 10 seconds a child, about 5 minutes; checklist: the wish is about the work and doable). Tools: page templates, the star-and-wish frame, the pretend class book and pretend pages, the animal-parents read-aloud (Curio Class writes them). Host: how animal parents help their young, s1-parent-offspring in science grade 1 unit 7 (days 56 to 61), or caring for birds, ela grade 1 unit 4 (days 120 to 157); stand-alone: "How do animal parents keep their babies fed and warm?" | Last because it gathers the year: the know and wonder sheet (unit 1), the answering page (unit 2) and the source line (unit 3) are what each child brings to their page; the star and wish needs a page to give advice on. Placed in weeks 21 to 27 to run beside science's parents and young unit and ELA's bird units, so the class book answers a real class question. It holds 7 days because the page is drafted, advised on and revised before the book is shared | Candidate: the continuum's grade 1 cells on beginning to respond to feedback to revise (the revision day) and on contributing to a group product that tells classmates and families (the class book may go home in turn, by the school's own rules) | Kind feedback: the star comes first, wishes are about the page, never the child, and the maker may say no to a wish; the teacher models a wish on a pretend page before any pair work. Animal parents: the case set keeps to feeding and warmth (birds, penguins, kangaroos), never a hunt or a lost young one. No family photo or family fact goes in the book. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its read-aloud and pictures (C12) |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1, mid-year | 2 | 1 | 1 | Week 13, one session, about 17 minutes for 30. It samples unit 1 on new cases: a sheet item, a know and wonder sheet on a topic picture not seen before (g1-what-i-know, about 8 minutes); a scanned pointing item, story cover or information cover for a named question (about 2 minutes); and a whisper walk on why (g1-story-or-fact-book, about 7 minutes). Unit 2, which ended the week before, is not re-tested: its end-of-unit result is reused unscored, as a class count of the children who met each move, written on this check's sheet before unit 2's checklist is thrown away, so no per-child record outlives its unit (APPROACH.md, K to 2). The respond day (week 14) reteaches the missed step in small groups from the check's sheet, ending with one fresh try |
| 2, year-end | 4 | 1 | 2 | Week 28, one session, about 18 minutes for 30, all sheet and pointing items so the whole class answers at once. It samples units 1 to 3 on new cases: a printed contents page and a question read aloud, circle the heading and the page number (unit 2, about 5 minutes); copy a new cover's title and author onto a sticky note (unit 3, about 4 minutes); a know and wonder sheet on a new topic (unit 1, about 7 minutes); point at the stop steps in order for a pretend case (unit 2, about 2 minutes). Unit 4, which ended in week 27, is reused unscored as a class count on the sheet, as at mid-year. Respond day 1 (week 29) reteaches the gaps in small groups from the sheet, ending with one fresh try. Respond day 2 (week 30) is the year's reflect step (the continuum's grade 1 reflect cells and APPROACH.md's self-check, taken for the year): the class reads its information book again, each child compares one "know" from a unit 1 sheet with what they know now, says one step they now do well and one wonder they still have, while the teacher takes a last try with any child the reteach did not reach. The teacher passes on to the grade 2 teacher a class-level note of the moves the class needs again, never a per-child record (the checklist rule) |

Placement: neither check falls in a week where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a grade 1 core check (weeks 14, 15, 16,
19 and 29 to 32: social studies 14 and 31, science 15 and 29, ELA 16 and 32, math 19 and 30). The mid-year check sits
in week 13, before that cluster, and its respond day in week 14 is not a check; the year-end check sits in week 28, so
its two respond days fall in weeks 29 and 30. Week 13 also holds the computer science grade 1 mid-year check
(frame.py calendar, 2026-11-25), and week 28 the ELD and physical education grade 1 year-end checks; the after-core rule forbids only the core weeks, and any stagger among
the after-core courses is the lead's one calendar pass (For the lead, item 3). Unit 4 runs to week 27, where the
health grade 1 year-end check sits; that week holds no library check.

## Cross-subject

Every lean is to a grade 1 core course, the same year, so each is on the availability calendar. Course days and weeks
for the other courses are as their frames state them and data/plans/calendar.csv places them; exact lesson-level
availability waits on their scopes and sequences, so each lean keeps its "if later" line. Library is not a core
course, so no core course assumes it (lesson 32).

### English language arts, grade 1

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest the other course teaches it | If ELA teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g1-what-i-know | e1-shared-research (shared research on a class question) | Unit 1, weeks 1 to 6 | ela grade 1 unit 2 (days 34 to 75), weeks 7 to 15: later than this unit | Unit 1 teaches the know and wonder sheet itself, spoken and drawn; ELA's shared research then meets a class that already says what it knows before looking |
| g1-story-or-fact-book | e1-stories-and-information (books that tell stories and books that inform) | Unit 1, weeks 1 to 6 | ela grade 1 unit 2 (days 34 to 75), weeks 7 to 15: later than this unit | Unit 1 teaches the story-or-information choice itself on two covers and a few pages, building on the kindergarten sort; it leans on ELA for nothing |
