## For the lead

1. **Row changes (L103), for data/progressions/library.csv notes:** g1-story-or-fact-book, add: "The continuum's
   grade 1 priority skill on how the library orders fiction and picture books is met here by the bin labels; shelf
   order comes with g2-catalog-lookup in grade 2, since many class book corners have no shelves in order."
   g1-device-stop-rule, add: "The continuum's grade 1 priority skill on a username that protects private information
   is met here as never typing a name or school; Curio Class sets up no student account, so no username is made."
   Pick: apply both; no id, assumes or hands_on changes.
2. **later.csv L103.** Grade 1 is done (the Check set read bullet and item 1). Pick: L103 stays open for grades 3 to
   12; grade 2 is in its own frame.
3. **After-core check stagger in grade 1.** Week 13 also holds the computer science mid-year check, and week 28 the
   ELD and physical education year-end checks. Pick:
   settle it in the one calendar pass across the after-core frames; this frame's checks move only then, and only to
   week 27 or earlier, since weeks 29 to 32 hold core checks.
4. **Unit 2's shared device.** g1-device-stop-rule names a shared class device; this frame gives it a full paper
   path (the card frame), so no school must own one. Pick: no row change.
Said plainly: rigor can be ranked, so where two instruments ask for more or less of the same thing, the higher
one can be followed. A values conflict is not always a matter of higher or lower. Two instruments can ask for
opposite things, and neither is the more rigorous. So the rule needs working out alongside (a): what "higher"
means, which conflicts it can settle, and what governs the ones it cannot. Until then FOUNDATION's promises still
bind, promise 7 among them, and C12 and the sensitivity read stay as they are.

