strand,grade,skill_id,standard,assumes,hands_on,model_in,model_out,words,cross_subject,notes
exp,K,k-wonder,AASL.V.C.1,,g1-what-i-know,,,wonder; question,social-studies:ssk-asking-questions (the same habit of asking about the world),"Children look closely at a picture, an object or a short read-aloud on a neutral topic (seeds, snails, rain) and say one ""I wonder..."" question of their own; the teacher writes each on a class wonder board that later units draw from. Checked alone in kindergarten: each child says a question, not a statement, about the day's topic, recorded on the teacher's checklist. Later grades carry it as they turn wonders into research questions."
eng,K,k-library-visit,~AASL.VI.A.1,,g1-device-stop-rule,,,library; borrow; return; shelf,,"How a library works and how to treat it: turning pages with clean hands, putting a book back where it lives, borrowing and returning on a set day. The class book corner stands in where there is no school library. On any day with a device, children learn the stop rule as three pictured words (close, tell, no blame). Teacher note: no library access is assumed; the print path is the class book corner. The adult checks every page a child sees and says what happens if something upsetting appears: the child closes it, tells, and is never blamed. The continuum's K cell names knowing that private information is never shared online; here it is taught in grade 1 (g1-device-stop-rule), the first year a device is used. Checked in kindergarten: the child puts a fetched book back in the bin it lives in and points at the three stop pictures in order."
cur,K,k-picture-book-labels,~AASL.IV.A.2; ~AASL.IV.B.1,,g1-story-or-fact-book,,,label; spine; story book; fact book,"ela:ek-books-and-authors (front cover, title page and the kinds of books)","Children find a book in the class book corner by its spine label (a picture or color sticker for each bin) and sort a small pile into story books and fact books by looking at the cover and a few pages. With help: the teacher names the bin, the child walks to it and brings back a book. Checked in kindergarten: the child brings back a book from a named bin. Carried here; the kinds of sources are checked alone in grade 2. The continuum's K priority skill names labels and ABC order of picture books; here the bins carry picture or color labels, since many class book corners have no ABC shelves, and shelf order comes with g2-catalog-lookup in grade 2. The continuum puts sorting books by fiction and nonfiction in grade 1; here it is done with help on a small pile and checked as story book or fact book, the K cells being that picture books can be true or not true and sorting things and information."
inq,K,k-listen-and-retell,~AASL.I.B.1,,g1-find-in-a-book,,,fact; find out,ela:ek-shared-research (recall information to answer a class question); science:sk-needs-places (host task: what an animal needs and where it lives),"The class holds one question (what does a rabbit eat?) while the teacher reads a fact book aloud; children raise a hand when they hear the answer and retell that one fact in their own words. The skill is listening for the part that answers, not every interesting part. With help, on a teacher-chosen book; carried in K to 3."
eng,K,k-who-made-book,~AASL.VI.B.2,,g1-author-and-title,,,author; illustrator; title,ela:ek-books-and-authors (naming the author and illustrator and what each does),"Before and after a read-aloud the class says who wrote the words and who made the pictures, and that a person made this book. Children point to the names on the cover. This is the first step of crediting a maker; a credit line written alone comes in grade 2. Telling a found fact (it came from the book's maker) from an idea of one's own, the continuum's K academic-integrity priority skill, is said aloud here."
inq,K,k-class-inquiry,~AASL.I.B.3; ~AASL.I.C.1,,g1-class-book; g1-star-and-wish,,research-cycle,ask; find; share,ela:ek-shared-research (a shared research and writing project),"The class runs the whole cycle once a unit on one wonder: ask (from the board), find (a read-aloud, a picture set, a visit from a staff member), share (each child draws one found fact with a label or dictated sentence and shows it in the circle) and reflect by pointing at the four pictured steps. Children listen to a friend's drawing and say one thing it taught them. Introduces the research cycle model as four pictures."
col,K,k-talk-in-group,~AASL.III.D.1; ~AASL.II.A.1,,g1-group-helpers,,,take turns; listen; idea,ela:ek-discussion (taking turns in a conversation); social-studies:ssk-talk-together (talking together in a group),"In a group of three around a picture set, each child takes a turn telling one fact they found while the others listen, then the group says what each friend added (""Sam found that...""). With help, using a talking object. Speaking up with findings is checked alone in grade 3."
inq,1,g1-what-i-know,AASL.I.A.2; ~AASL.V.C.1,k-wonder,g2-question-to-research,,,know; wonder; learn,ela:e1-shared-research (shared research on a class question); science:s1-parent-offspring (host task: how animal parents help their young),"Before a read-aloud or picture set, each child draws or says one thing they already know about the topic and one thing they wonder, on a two-box know and wonder sheet; after, they mark any ""know"" that turned out not quite right. Checked alone in grade 1: the child gives a real prior fact about the topic, unprompted. Grade 1 owns it because the move is small and scorable; later grades carry it into research plans."
cur,1,g1-story-or-fact-book,~AASL.IV.A.2,k-picture-book-labels,g2-kinds-of-sources,,,information book; story book; true,ela:e1-stories-and-information (the difference between books that tell stories and books that inform),"Given a question (how tall do sunflowers grow?), children choose between a story book and an information book and say why the information book is more likely to answer it, then find the fact-book bin by its label. With help in grade 1; grade 2 names more kinds of source and checks it alone. The continuum's grade 1 priority skill on how the library orders fiction and picture books is met here by the bin labels; shelf order comes with g2-catalog-lookup in grade 2, since many class book corners have no shelves in order."
cur,1,g1-find-in-a-book,~AASL.IV.B.1,k-listen-and-retell,g2-catalog-lookup; g2-fact-sort; g2-two-books-differ,,,contents; heading; label; picture,ela:e1-text-features (text features that help find information),"Children use the cover, the contents page, headings, pictures and labels to find the page in an information book that answers a class question, then show the page to a partner. The skill is searching inside one source for an answer instead of reading from the start. With help, on a teacher-chosen book."
eng,1,g1-author-and-title,~AASL.VI.B.2,k-who-made-book,g2-who-made-it; g2-credit-line,,,title; author; made by,,"When a child shares a fact, they say where it came from: ""I found this in the book (title) by (author)."" The teacher models it every share; children copy title and author from the cover onto a sticky note with help. Carried here; the credit line is checked alone in grade 2."
inq,1,g1-class-book,~AASL.I.B.3; ~AASL.I.C.1,k-class-inquiry,g2-research-share,research-cycle,research-cycle,page; class book; share,ela:e1-shared-research (a shared research and writing project),"The class runs the research cycle on one question and makes a class information book, one page per child: a labeled drawing and a sentence of a found fact. The book is read aloud to the class and each child says one thing a friend's page taught them. Grade 1 adds the product (a page that answers part of the question) to the kindergarten drawing."
inq,1,g1-star-and-wish,~AASL.I.C.2,k-class-inquiry,g2-research-share,,,star; wish; kind,,"Children give a classmate one star (something the page does well, such as a clear label) and one wish (something to add) using a pictured frame, then the maker chooses whether to use the wish. The skill is advice about the work, kind and specific, not ""I like it."" With help; checked alone in grade 3."
col,1,g1-group-helpers,~AASL.III.D.1; ~AASL.II.A.1,k-talk-in-group,g2-group-research,,,job; helper; team,ela:e1-discussion (building on others' talk in conversations),"Groups of three take pictured jobs (finder, holder, teller) to find one fact in a picture set, swap jobs the next time, and at the end name what each helper added. Grade 1 adds jobs to the kindergarten turn-taking."
eng,1,g1-device-stop-rule,~AASL.VI.A.1,k-library-visit,g2-catalog-lookup,,,device; stop; tell,,"On a shared class device, children use only the page the teacher opened and practice the stop rule on pretend cases (a pop-up, a picture that feels wrong): close it, tell the adult, no blame. They also learn never to type their name or school. Teacher note: open search is not used in grade 1; the teacher page says what the adult does if an unsafe result appears and points to the health steps if a child tells you something worrying. The continuum's grade 1 priority skill on a username that protects private information is met here as never typing a name or school; Curio Class sets up no student account, so no username is made."
inq,2,g2-question-to-research,~AASL.I.A.1; ~AASL.I.A.2,g1-what-i-know,g3-research-questions,,,who; what; where; when; why; how,"social-studies:ss1-supporting-questions (questions that help answer a bigger question); ela:e2-wh-questions (asking and answering who, what, where, when, why and how)","With the teacher, the class picks a question from the wonder board that a book could answer and pairs write one question-word question each about it, after saying what they already know. The teacher sorts them; children see which ones a book could answer. With help; research questions are written alone in grade 3. Each child first writes an 'I wonder' of their own on a board card, the continuum's grade 2 priority skill on wonders made independently, before the pair writes its question with help."
cur,2,g2-kinds-of-sources,AASL.IV.A.2,g1-story-or-fact-book,g3-source-kinds-and-makers,,,source; encyclopedia; dictionary; map; expert,,"Children match a question to the kind of source likely to answer it: an information book, a children's encyclopedia, a picture dictionary, a children's magazine, a map or a globe, a person who knows (the school nurse, a gardener), or a page the teacher chose. Checked alone in grade 2: given three new questions, the child picks a fitting kind for each and says why. Owned here; later grades choose among sources (IV.A.3, grade 5). A page the teacher chose stands in for the designated online encyclopedias and databases of the continuum's grade 2 cell, since no device or database is assumed."
cur,2,g2-catalog-lookup,~AASL.IV.B.1; ~AASL.VI.A.1,g1-find-in-a-book; g1-device-stop-rule,g3-key-words; g3-safe-searching,,,catalog; call number; shelf order,,"Children look up a book by title or author in a printed or invented catalog screen, copy its call label, and find it on the shelf by letter and number order. A practice day uses a shelf of mixed-up books to put in order. On a device they use the stop rule. Checked in grade 2 as the grade's priority skill: the child finds a named book from a printed catalog entry by title or author and shelf order. The continuum lists key-word lookup in K to 2; library leaves key words to grade 3, when children can type or spell them, and owns IV.B.1 at grade 4, where more than one kind of source is searched. Found books go back in shelf order and borrowed books come back on the set day, and any device day follows the school's own device rules (the continuum's grade 2 priority skills on library rules and acceptable computer use)."
cur,2,g2-who-made-it,~AASL.IV.B.3,g1-author-and-title,g3-source-kinds-and-makers,,,maker; purpose; sell; teach,social-studies:ss2-source-maker (who made a source and why); ela:e2-author-purpose (the main purpose of a text),"For a book or a teacher-chosen invented web page, children name who made it (a writer, a museum, a store) and why: to teach, to tell a story, to sell. Social studies asks who made a source from the past; library asks it of the books and pages students use to find facts, and both are taught in grade 2. The continuum lists naming a site's maker in K to 2; here it is done with help on invented pages, and checked alone with the first lateral check in grade 5, because a K to 2 check of a real site would need open search."
eng,2,g2-credit-line,AASL.VI.B.2,g1-author-and-title,g3-source-list,,citation,credit; title; author,,"Every product ends with a credit line for each source: title and author, copied correctly from the cover or the page. Checked alone in grade 2: the child writes a correct credit line for a new book. Owned here as the first honest-use move; grades 3 to 12 grow it into a source list, a bibliography and a set citation style. Introduces the citation model (the credit line is its simplest form)."
cur,2,g2-fact-sort,~AASL.IV.B.4,g1-find-in-a-book,g3-notes-in-boxes,,,sort; group; fact,science:s2-water-where (host task: where water is found on Earth),"Children sort found facts, drawn or written on cards, into picture boxes that match the question's parts (where water is, what shape it takes, where it goes). The teacher sets the boxes; children say why a card belongs. With help; notes in boxes they plan themselves come in grade 3. Each card holds a few words or a drawing, never a copied sentence (the continuum's grade 2 priority skill); key-word notes the student plans come in grade 3."
inc,2,g2-two-books-differ,~AASL.II.B.2,g1-find-in-a-book,g3-source-kinds-and-makers,,,same; different; check,social-studies:ss2-two-accounts (comparing two accounts of the same thing); ela:e2-compare-texts (comparing two texts on the same topic),"Two books on the same topic give a different fact (how long a turtle lives, how tall a tree grows). Children find the two facts, say what is the same and what differs, and decide to check a third source. Teacher note: K to 5 case sets stay neutral, so the differences are facts about animals, plants, weather and places, never views on politics, religion, identity groups or conflict."
inq,2,g2-research-share,~AASL.I.B.3; ~AASL.I.C.1; ~AASL.I.D.4,g1-class-book; g1-star-and-wish,g3-make-product; g3-gallery-feedback,research-cycle,research-cycle,poster; share; look back,ela:e2-research (a shared research project),"Pairs run the cycle on a class question and make a poster with two found facts, a picture and a credit line, share it with another pair, and say back what the other poster taught them. At the end each child points to the pictured step that went best and the one to do better. Grade 2 adds a pair product and the spoken look-back."
col,2,g2-group-research,~AASL.III.D.1; ~AASL.II.A.1; ~AASL.II.D.2,g1-group-helpers,g3-research-team,,,interview; ask; listen; team,social-studies:ss2-family-interviews (gathering information by asking people),"A research group plans two questions about the class topic (how plants are cared for, what a school job involves) to ask another person (a classmate, a school staff member, or a family member at home), asks them, and brings back the answers; the group then says what each member added. Teacher note: interview rules, APPROACH Sensitive ground 8; a family interview is optional and a staff member can stand in."
inq,3,g3-research-questions,AASL.I.A.1; ~AASL.IV.A.1,g2-question-to-research,g4-narrow-and-plan; g4-own-question,research-cycle,research-cycle,research question; topic; thick question; thin question,social-studies:ss3-inquiry-questions (compelling and supporting questions); ela:e3-short-research (short research projects),"From a class topic, after a short background read from a source the teacher gives, each student writes three research questions of their own, sorts them into thin (one fact) and thick (needs several sources), and picks one a source could answer. They also sort a set of questions into ""I know it"", ""I need to look it up"" and ""nobody can look this up"". Checked alone in grade 3: three real research questions on a new topic. Owned here; later grades narrow and plan them."
