# The adoption bar for the other subjects

**Status:** second draft, 2026-11-11. Revised once after the curriculum expert's cold read
(docs/reviews/bar-subjects-2026-11-11.md). The owner's answers were applied on 2026-11-11 (Desk card
q-bar-subjects; see "Decided" and "Still open with Cory" near the end). Not read by the expert since revision.

**Core subjects come first.** This is a soft goal (Cory, 2026-11-11; docs/agents/decisions/BASELINE.md rule 24).
Work on the subjects on this page waits behind math, English language arts, science and social studies unless it
is complementary, such as a shared rule or a standards fix that serves both. Social studies is core. Every course
is still finished to the bar; this sets the order, not the scope.

**What this page is for.** Every one of the 120 courses (118 active, 2 world languages shelved) is to be finished to the adoption bar (docs/FOUNDATION.md,
section 4; docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md). Math has a rubric file; English language arts and science rubrics are drafted
separately. This page writes the bar for every other subject: one shared core, then a short part per subject.

**What we may never say.** That Curio Class was reviewed by, meets, or is endorsed by any body named here. This is a
bar we hold ourselves to. Most instruments are all rights reserved, so nothing is quoted. Every criterion is in our
own words.

**What this page is not.** An approach page. Layer 3 asks for one per subject before a scope and sequence, and none exists (see
"Before a scope and sequence in this subject"). A subject's part moves to its own docs/RUBRIC-*.md file once it is firm.

## The pass rule

**Accepted for now (Cory, 2026-11-11), to be tested on the first finished course.** No course has been read
against it yet, so it may prove too hard or too easy.
It copies the shape of the instruments read: alignment, accuracy and feasibility are a door that stops a review, and
the rest is scored.

- **The door: C1, C2, C3 and C14, each passed in full.** A course that fails one is not read further until fixed.
- **C4 to C13, C15, C16:** each is marked pass, part pass or fail. A finished course has no fail and no more than
  three part passes.
- **Pass:** no "fails if" condition is found in the sample. **Part pass:** a condition is found in one sampled unit
  only, and the fix is local. **Fail:** it is found in two or more units, or is built into the course's design.
- **The sample.** The reviewer reads the whole scope and sequence and coverage table, then three whole units picked by the
  reviewer, never by the builder, one from each third of the year: every lesson, page, tool and check in them. A
  course of four units or fewer is read whole. Below, "three units" means this sample.
- The subject part is read the same way: no criterion failed, and the part pass count above covers core and subject
  criteria together.
- **A small course is read the same way.** SEL and library skills courses may be smaller than other courses by
  the nature of the subject (decided 2026-11-11). They are "held to the whole core at the size its scope and sequence states":
  every criterion from C1 to C16 and every criterion of the subject part still applies, and what is measured is
  the number of units and days the scope and sequence itself sets out, not the size of a math or science course. Being small
  waives no criterion. **Smaller means fewer minutes a week, never fewer units** (Cory 2026-11-15, later.csv L43): each
  SEL and library course runs the whole year at one or two sessions a week, or about one every two weeks in the two library band courses (its frame states which), so every standard
  of its framework is still taught, practised spaced out over the year and checked. This fits how schools staff these
  subjects (a counselor or librarian seeing a class once a week).
- **Beyond the three units,** the reviewer reads: for C1, the whole coverage table, tracing ten standards of the
  reviewer's choice to lesson and check item; for C4, the progression page; for C8 and C9, one cumulative check;
  for C13, the approach page; for C3 and C16, all public copy for the course and the logic model.

## The shared core

Layers are those in docs/ROADMAP.md, "The layers": 1 foundation, 2 standards, 3 approach, 4 progressions, 5 scope and sequence,
6 unit plan and section plan, 7 lessons, tools, print and checks. The rows recur across the instruments read in full (see
the sources table). No count per row is given, because the first draft's "Seen in" column was not reliable.

| # | A reviewer looks for | What shows it in a Curio Class course | Layer | Fails if |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| C1 | **Every standard of the course is named and taught in full,** at the depth its wording asks, with little time on content from outside the course | The scope and sequence's standards table with days; a coverage table pointing each standard to lesson, tool and check item; a named edition loaded in data/standards/ | 2, 5 | Any standard lacks a lesson, practice or a check item. A traced standard is taught below its wording. More than 10 percent of teaching days go to content outside the course's standards (our number, reset per subject once its scope and sequence form is known). The edition is not named or not loaded |
| C2 | **A year plan with reasons:** units in order, why, days per unit, minutes per lesson, a shorter path | The scope and sequence: every teaching day, the reason for the order, the stated year and period length, the short path, gap days | 5 | A teaching day has no plan. The order has no stated reason. No minutes per lesson. No short path. The days do not add up to the stated year |
| C3 | **Nothing wrong, nothing sold, authors plain.** (a) Facts, figures, terms and pictures are correct and current, with sources. (b) No product, brand or provider is promoted; opinion is marked. (c) Who wrote it, and how, is stated | The accuracy read at the Finish check, with the reader named; a source line on each fact page; public copy that names no companies and says how the course was made. Health: see its criterion 7 | 7 | (a) An uncorrected error in a key, a definition or a figure; a dated figure with no date or source. (b) Any promotion. (c) No statement of authorship |
| C4 | **Each idea rests on the last:** what earlier grades taught, how units link, what comes next | The subject's progression page; the scope and sequence's builds-on and comes-back columns; the readiness check or opening retrieval task on each unit plan | 4, 5, 6 | A unit leans on an idea nothing earlier taught and no readiness check or retrieval task catches it. No progression page. A band course does not say what the band before taught |
| C5 | **A unit overview that prepares the teacher:** purpose, background knowledge, the unit's words, how it is assessed | The section plan's fields; the teacher page that teaches the teacher | 6, 7 | The teacher page restates the lessons and adds no background. No word list. Assessment not stated |
| C6 | **Lessons ready to teach:** objective, questions, tasks, materials, time per part, grouping | Daily plans and student pages for every scope and sequence day; the supplies list; the projector path on tool days | 7 | A scope and sequence day has no plan or no student page. A lesson lacks an objective, materials or times. The teacher must write the questions or tasks |
| C7 | **New things are taught, then practised with fading help,** spread over time, and they come back | The section plan field naming who holds the model each day and the day it is withdrawn; the plan for what earlier work returns | 3, 6 | A new skill is first met in independent work. No day is named for withdrawing the model. Nothing from earlier units returns |
| C8 | **An assessment system:** checks before, during and after teaching, each tied to named standards, more than one kind of task, doing where the subject is a doing subject, and students judging and tracking their own work | The unaided check in each tool, readiness checks and end-of-unit assessments on each unit plan (where a unit assumes only rows used the year before or earlier the same year, its opening retrieval task's teacher notes, named in the unit plan, are the before-teaching evidence), cumulative checks on the scope and sequence; a student self-check page per unit | 6, 7 | One of the three sizes is missing. An item names no standard. A doing subject is checked by recall alone. Students never judge or track their own work in a unit |
| C9 | **The teacher can score it and act on it:** keys, rubrics, scored samples, what each wrong answer shows, what to do next | Keys that explain each wrong answer and the reteach; a rubric and sample pieces for every open task; the respond day; the class record sheet | 6, 7 | A key gives answers only. An open task has no rubric, or samples at fewer than two levels. No next step. No record sheet |
| C10 | **Every learner works on the course's content:** help for students behind that keeps the goal, deeper extensions, guidance for disabilities, a reading load that fits the grade with help for students who read below it | The three hooks on every section plan; the reteach per readiness gap; extension on the same standard; the section plan's reading load field | 3, 6, 7 | A support lowers the goal or swaps the content. No extension on the same standard. No guidance for disabilities. Reading load field empty, or pages above grade with no help |
| C11 | **English learners are supported inside the lesson:** the language the lesson asks for, key words in depth, frames, levels where the subject can, and help telling a language gap from a content gap | The English learner hook in every tool and teacher page; Word Wall cards; a line in each key on answers that show a language gap | 6, 7 | Supports are a general list not tied to the lesson. No frames. Nothing helps the teacher tell a language gap from a content gap |
| C12 | **People are shown with care and range:** many voices and places, no stereotypes, no shaming, hard facts told plainly | The fresh-agent sensitivity read (FOUNDATION promise 7); a record per unit of whose work and voices appear | 1, 7 | A stereotype or shaming passes. One group carries all the examples. A hard fact is softened until it is false. No read on record |
| C13 | **The approach is explained, and families are told** | The subject's approach page, with sources and how much of each was read; a family note per unit, in English and Spanish, written when the unit is finished | 3, 7 | No approach page. A research claim rests on a source nobody read. A sampled unit has no family note |
| C14 | **It can be run in an ordinary room:** time, space, supplies, cost and teacher knowledge are stated and modest | The supplies list with cheap stand-ins; the paper path for every tool day; time per lesson; a free site | 1, 5, 7 | The course needs space, kit, time or training most schools lack and gives no stand-in. A cost to the school is not stated. A tool day has no paper path |
| C15 | **Screens serve the learning:** tied to the objective, run on school devices, usable without mouse or colour, private, with a non-screen path | The tool's named job in the section plan; the access check; single-file offline tools that store nothing off the device | 6, 7 | A tool has no named job. It fails keyboard-only or colour-free play. It needs an install, an account or a network. It stores data off the device |
| C16 | **Honest about evidence** | A one-page logic model per course; the plain statement that the course has not been tried with children | 1, 3 | Any claim of effect. No logic model. Research cited that nobody on the project read |

**Where our promises cost us marks, by choice.** Curio Class will not build some things rubrics ask for
(FOUNDATION, section 3): data dashboards (Texas names one as an example), adapting to each student and plugging
into a learning management system (Ohio, narrative items), accessible-format files lodged with a national centre
(Oregon), and professional learning tied to the materials (Ohio, scored). Our answers are the class record sheet,
score codes, open pages, print and teacher pages. Professional learning: none for now (decided 2026-11-11). It is
a future area of growth, not a closed door.

**Where the instruments conflict on values.** Oklahoma's arts rubric checks materials against state law. Maryland,
the federal health tool and the SEL registry ask for equity content in terms that pull the other way. No stance
passes all of them. C12 follows FOUNDATION promise 7, a middle line, and Curio Class does not claim to satisfy any
state's values criteria. Said once here, so it is not re-opened per course. Cory's values rule (2026-11-12) now
settles these conflicts in order: the higher court, then the evidence, then dignity and openness, then the age of
the student (see "Still open with Cory", b, and social studies item 11). FOUNDATION's promises still bind.

**Acceptability is local.** A national site cannot settle what suits each community, so each course page says what
a school may leave out. For health this is decided: all sensitive topics are carried, and each sensitive unit can
be left out without breaking the course.

## The subject parts
### 11. Library and research skills. Ours, untested

Decided 2026-11-11: these stay separate courses. The bar below is our own, named as ours, and it may be small.
The courses may be smaller than other courses by the nature of the subject (see the pass rule for what that
means).

**Rests on.** No materials instrument exists. Built from the Empire State continuum (all four band files read in
full: 3 to 5 on 2026-11-16, PK to 2, 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 on 2026-11-21), the national school library standards
(second edition chart read 2026-11-21) and one trial of lateral reading known through a press notice.
*To firm it:* read the trial itself.

1. [O] An inquiry cycle is taught in every band: ask, find, judge, use, share, reflect.
2. [O] Each grade has named priority skills, each with a check.
3. [O, one trial] Judging a source is taught as leaving the page to see what others say about it, not as a
   checklist run on the page.
4. [O] Skills are practised on real tasks from the grade's other courses, named in the scope and sequence.
5. [O] Citing, fair use and honest use of others' work are taught at each band.
6. [O] Students make a product from their research and share it.

**Fails if:** a one-off orientation; a website checklist; skills with no task to use them on.

**Unsupported.** Everything beyond the trial. No evidence for the elementary grades.

**Standards.** data/standards/aasl-2018.txt: the second edition's 68 ids (unchanged from 2018; the file name and
the `AASL-2018` tag stay as names, the header says why). Our own words: data/standards/aasl-words.txt.

**Scope and sequence states:** the host tasks from other courses. **Section plan fills:** the check per skill.
**Confidence:** thin.

# Library and research skills: approach (layer 3)

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-21 (Desk q-prework-open-2). Written 2026-11-16, read cold and fixed 2026-11-20 (docs/reviews/prework-library-read-2026-11-20.md).** Short by design: it fills the subject
slots of the unit and section plan (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md) and leans on the shared approach (docs/APPROACH.md),
the lens (docs/agents/lenses/library.md) and the standard (STANDARD.md). Ours, untested. K to 5 courses meet one or two
sessions a week all year (Cory, 2026-11-15); the 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 courses about one session every two weeks
across their grades (Cory, 2026-11-16); the frame says which. Fixed again 2026-11-21 from the second read and
calibration (docs/reviews/prework-library-fixes-2026-11-20.md). Fields not named on this page (unit field 1,
section field 2) and the shared parts of the named ones are filled as UNIT-PLAN.md says, with nothing added for
library.

## The kinds of day, in library's words

| Shared kind | In library | What it looks like |
|---|---|---|
| Understanding | Show-me day | A skill modeled on the host task: the teacher thinks aloud through a search, a lateral check or a citation |
| Fluency | Practice day | Short, varied tries of the skill with feedback: three claims checked laterally, five sources cited |
| Application | Inquiry day | Students run part of the cycle on the host task themselves: question, search, judge, note, make, share |
| Check | Assessment day | A search, judgment or citation actually done on a new case, scored on a short rubric, plus a few items |
| Respond | Respond day | The product or notes revised from feedback |

A unit runs at least one full inquiry cycle on its host task, with the product shared. Each unit names the kind of
host task and one example from the grade's core courses (by progression row id), for the teacher to schedule
against, and also carries a stand-alone host task of its own (an invented question with a closed source set), so the
unit can run when no core task is in step, or at home where a family uses no other Curio Class course. The scope and
sequence records both.

## The form of daily evidence

- **Show-me and practice days:** an exit ticket that asks the student to do the skill once on a new case (check a
  claim and say what other sources said; fix a citation), with error letters for the usual slips.
- **Inquiry days:** the working notes (question, search terms tried, sources with a judgment line each).
- **K to 2:** the teacher's observation of one named skill per child, on a class checklist kept by the teacher and
  thrown away at the end of the unit, plus a drawing or a spoken answer.
- **Once a unit, a self-check** (the cycle's reflect step; bar C8): after the product is shared, the student rates
  their own use of the skill card's steps and names one thing to do differently next time (K to 2: pointing at
  pictured steps and saying it aloud).

## What library adds to the plan fields

- Unit field 2: the host task and its course (and the stand-alone host task, above); the AASL ids the unit owns,
  each with its phrase from data/standards/aasl-words.txt, never the competency text (STANDARD.md section 1), and
  the grade's priority skills in our own words; the product and its audience; the day the skill card is withdrawn.
- Unit field 3 (where it sits): builds on (the earlier library row by id, and any ELA or social studies row it
  leans on, from its `cross_subject`); leads to; comes back in.
- Unit field 4 (readiness): one short item on the skill the unit builds on (find the maker of a page; name a
  source's kind); the ten-minute reteach is the earlier unit's show-me case.
- Unit field 5 (end-of-unit assessment): a search, a judgment or a citation actually done on a new case, scored on
  a short rubric, plus a few items. Never a quiz on the names of the steps alone.
- Unit field 6 (error register): the usual slips (trusting the look of a page, staying on the page to judge it,
  copying instead of noting, a citation missing its date or maker).
- Unit field 7 (response allocation): the rubric is teacher-judged, with no hands-up self-marking, and the respond
  day's groups come from the class record sheet by the rubric step missed; items with one answer are checked on the
  page.
- Unit field 8 (tools and supplies): printed source sets, invented pages and search results, the research notebook;
  no product or service names.
- Unit field 9 (family note): in English and Spanish: the skill's name and steps and one thing to try together at
  home (checking who is behind a page you both use); if a school leaves the unit out, the ids not claimed that
  year (STANDARD.md section 4).
- Unit field 10 (sources): what was read and how much, what is from memory, no claim of effect; the record of whose
  work and voices the unit's source set shows (C12; Sensitive ground, item 5); a line pointing to the course-level
  logic model (C16).
- Section field 1: before you teach: the AASL ids and the priority skill, the skill as an adult uses it, and why the
  method beats a checklist of a page's looks. What the teacher page must explain: the sensitive ground the section
  touches (below); on a day with open search, the stop rule and what the adult does if an unsafe result appears; and,
  if a student tells you something or a result shows harm to a child, health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md,
  "Standards and the teacher page").
- Section field 3: each day's evidence in the form above.
- Section field 4: expected slips beside a sound example.
- Section field 5 (what is carried, and instruments): the unit's skill card (the steps of the one search, check or
  citation move the unit teaches). The teacher holds it on the first show-me day, thinking aloud on an invented
  case; pairs hold it on the first practice day; it is withdrawn on the first inquiry day, which the section plan
  names. The unit check is taken without it. A skill returns in later units with the card for one day, then without
  it. K to 2 use the same steps as three or four pictured words said aloud. A room with nothing: the print path
  (printed source sets, invented pages and search results), with no library access assumed.
- Section field 6: the kind of day in library's words; a day opens with one earlier skill tried on a short case or
  a source prompt, five minutes at most; a new skill is shown on a show-me day before students try it (rule 13).
- Section field 7 (talk and writing): a search-aloud routine (one student types or points, one says what they see,
  one records), and sentence frames for a judgment ("I trust this less because...").
- Section field 8 (practice that returns): one earlier skill, named, tried once on a new case.
# Curio Class: the foundation (layer 1)

Gathered 2026-11-11 (Cory). This is the one home for what Curio Class is for, who it serves, what it promises and
what it assumes. Every layer above it (the approach to teaching, the progressions, the scopes and sequences, the section plans, the
tools) is built on this page and may not contradict it. Until today these lines were spread over docs/VISION.md,
docs/ROADMAP.md, docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md and docs/agents/decisions/BASELINE.md; those files keep their detail and now
point here. Where one of them disagrees with this page, this page wins and the other file is fixed.

It changes rarely, and only with Cory. The layers and the order of work are in docs/ROADMAP.md ("The layers").

## 1. What it is for

A free, complete, open K-12 classroom toolkit: interactive tools, printables, lessons and units for every standard,
so a teacher can run a full course without paying for curriculum or software (VISION, Mission).

**The end goal.** A teacher can run any course on Curio Class from the first day to the last, free, with no account,
and the course would stand up to a state adoption review. A course is a grade of a subject or a high school course.
Every core subject is planned top down (progression, course frames, scopes and sequences, unit plans) before days
are written (docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md, Cory 2026-11-13); each course is finished as a whole before it is called
finished. The earlier pilot-first bend (2026-11-11) is retired; the probe and guard replace it.

**We own the full course** (Cory, 2026-11-11; base texts added 2026-11-12). Curio Class owns every course: its
shape, tone, format, gates and reads. Where a well-reviewed open program carries a licence as open as ours (CC BY
or public domain), it can be the course's **base text**: adapted into Curio Class days, molded to our needs and
voice, with a credit line that says it was changed (math: Illustrative Mathematics). Programs under non-commercial
or share-alike terms are evidence only, for order, time, models and anchors, never their wording or problems,
so every page stays CC BY 4.0. We do not stitch two programs together: each hangs together through its own routines and pictures, and a
mix is weaker than either. A tool built to a clear job in our unit will also fit the same lesson in a course a
teacher already uses, and that is welcome, but it is not the plan.

## 2. Who it is for

| Who | What that means for the work |
|---|---|
| **A classroom teacher** running a course | The main reader of every scope and sequence, section plan and teacher page. Teacher pages teach the teacher |
| **A parent teaching one child at home** (named 2026-11-11; how far, decided 2026-11-12) | The second audience, served by the same course, never a separate track. Every lesson day carries an "at home" block (a 30 to 40 minute one-child path, what the parent says where the class has partner talk, which items to do, home swaps for materials); a course gives placement from its readiness checks, a numbered calendar ("36-week" in Cory's 2026-11-12 call; the school calendar is 160 days over 32 weeks, docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) and a record that works as a portfolio; answer keys explain, not only list; sample pieces show what grade-level work looks like. Not planned: separate home courses or multi-age plans |
| **A student alone** | The site is public and a child may open any tool with no adult present. Every tool stands alone: defaults work for a solo user and teacher toggles are extras (BASELINE rule 4) |

## 3. The promises

These hold even where a review, a district system or a convenience would reward breaking them.

1. **Free for good.** No paywall, ever (ROADMAP, Goal).
2. **No accounts, no ads, no tracking, no student analytics** (VISION, "What we will not do"). A tool cannot
   remember a student from one day to the next and there is no live teacher dashboard. We chose that.
3. **Results live with the student and the teacher.** A score code the student shows, and printable record sheets.
   The score code records only what a tool can mark (ROADMAP, "What a finished course holds", item 4).
4. **Every tool works offline as a single file.** Where a tool stores anything, it says on screen that it never
   leaves the device (BASELINE rule 7).
5. **Paper is a first-class product.** Worksheets, units and printables matter as much as screens. Printables read in
   black and white.
6. **Honest words.** We say taught, practiced or shown, per the ledger, never more. Practice tools are not
   diagnoses and score codes are not proof. We never say Curio Class was reviewed by, meets or is endorsed by
   anyone. Public copy names no companies (BASELINE rule 15).
7. **Classroom-safe, and plain about hard facts.** History and science are not softened; they are told with care
   for the people involved, and a fresh agent checks sensitive content before it goes live (BASELINE rules 5, 6, 8).
8. **We do not copy a branded product** in name, look or text, and we respect every licence: read and link, adapt
   only where the licence allows.

## 4. The bar

State adoption is the bar we build to (Cory, 2026-11-10; BASELINE rule 20). A full course should meet the substance
of what state and national curriculum reviews ask for. We do not pursue formal adoption now. The bar applies to
courses and the shelves that feed them; Curiosities and Recess are outside it. The bar never outranks the promises
above. What it asks and what we took from it: docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md. The rubrics we read ourselves against are filed
as docs/RUBRIC-*.md.

**Quality over speed, and over saved work** (BASELINE rules 1, 2, 14). Each piece is really good before the next
wave grows. An existing spec or tool carries no weight against what a unit needs: if the unit needs something
different, the old work loses.

## 5. What we assume about the room

Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory) unless marked.

1. **Grades 3 to 12: one device per student on a tool day.** The courses are designed that way and say so plainly.
   Every tool day still carries one line for a single projector, because some rooms have nothing else and a reviewer
   will ask.
2. **Kindergarten to grade 2: one shared classroom device**, always with a paper path (Cory, 2026-11-14).
3. **The physical world is written into the lesson plan.** A tool does not replace rolling the marble, using a
   protractor or reading a whole book. Hands-on work, materials and their cost are part of the unit, with a path for
   a teacher who has nothing (docs/reviews/science-4-spine-2026-11-11.md, finding 7).
4. **A teacher judges open work.** A tool marks what has a right answer. For writing, argument and discussion, the
   tool gives the work a structure so it is quick to read, and print carries the rest: a rubric, sample pieces at
   each score level, and a look-for sheet. Our job is to make that judging fast and consistent (BASELINE rule 21
   already says a tool cannot mark writing or talk).
5. **Time.** A scope and sequence states its own year and period length and gives a shorter path where ordinary schools have less
   time than a full program assumes.

## 6. What a tool is for

Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory), from the discussion recorded in docs/agents/decisions/DESK-ANSWERS.md.

1. **A tool is the end piece of instruction, a means to a defined end.** The end is defined in a section plan: what
   each day is for, where a tool is used and for what job. **No tool is built or refined until a section plan names
   its job.**
2. **A tool can be whatever the unit needs.** The section plan asks, for each key moment, what the ideal instrument for
   that idea would be. Sometimes that is a tool nobody has specced; sometimes it is a marble and a ramp. The limits
   are the promises and assumptions on this page and the cost of doing a tool well, not what software can do.
3. **Key tools and light tools.** A key tool is the one a day is planned around: it does the day's main job in a
   way print cannot (feedback on each try, help that fades as the student succeeds, many cases fast), and the paper
   path is the fallback (reworded 2026-11-11; it had read "one the unit cannot run without", which no tool can meet
   while every plan must also run on paper). Key tools are built first and hold the course up. Light tools (called
   practice tools before 2026-11-11) are lighter, built from shared templates over the long term, and still correct
   and still meet the three hooks. Lighter never means lower quality. A course is runnable when its key tools are
   live and print covers the rest. "Key" is a mark given by the section plan; the roles of BASELINE rule 21 (teach,
   see why, practice) still say what a tool does.
4. **Curiosities and Recess** are play and wonder. They claim no standard and are outside the bar (VISION).
5. **Tools and specs made before this page are sorted by demand** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 26). Named by a
   section plan: course work. Live, passing the teaching test, not named: stays on the shelf, free-standing, and
   claims no course coverage. Everything else: frozen. A script makes the list; nobody argues a tool into a course.

## 7. How the work is checked

1. **The curriculum expert reads cold, often** (docs/agents/EXPERT.md). Cory's attention and a teacher's are the
   scarce things, so frequent cold reads by the expert, which teaches itself from outside sources first, are the
   main review at every layer. It is the main read, not the only one (changed 2026-11-11, when Cory accepted the cold strategic review). The
   writer and the expert are the same kind of model, so a cold read removes anchoring but not a shared mistake.
   Three things stand beside it: the sources the expert must open before it judges (the pedagogy and standards
   data, which Cory expects to grow), a script for everything that can be computed (item 2), a Codex read of a
   different model kind, and the agent reads in item 3.
2. **A machine checks what a machine can** (BASELINE rule 27). Standard codes, day counts, coverage of every part
   of a standard, and every answer that can be computed. A key the script cannot reproduce fails the build.
3. **Reads beside the expert** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 28; changed 2026-11-14). Every row below is
   required before the course it touches is called finished. On 2026-11-14 Cory said there is no outside support
   ("We have no support. Our agents are our best hope for success"): the content rows are agent reads, each by a
   fresh agent that studies its sources first (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md). No outside human reader is
   planned. Pilots are retired (2026-11-13): the probe and guard (docs/pipeline/PROBE-GUARD.md) take their place.

   | Who | What they read or do | When |
   |---|---|---|
   | The expert agent (was a working teacher on the pilot unit) | Reads every plan and the probe and guard days against the subject lens and rubric | Every unit plan; the probe and the guard |
   | The answer-key agent (was a math content reviewer) | Solves every item of one whole course's checks cold and compares with the key, beside the script check | Before a math course is called finished |
   | The safety agent (was a science teacher) | Safety and materials of every investigation, against published school science safety guidance; and every PE unit with striking tools, mats, climbing, resistance training or an outdoor trip, against published school PE safety guidance (Cory, 2026-11-21) | Before a science course is called finished; before such a PE unit goes live |
   | The rights agent (was a second reader on rights) | The licence and source of every text a course teaches from | Before an ELA text is taught |
   | The Spanish agent (was a native Spanish reader) | Family notes, and student materials if they are made | Before Spanish is published in a course |
   | The sensitivity agent (was a health specialist) | The sensitive health topics, against public health guidance | Before those units go live |
   | The sensitivity agent (was a reader from the community concerned) | Native, cultural, disability and hard-history content, after studying the community's own published voice | Before those units go live |

   Rights and Spanish became agent reads on 2026-11-14 (DESK-ANSWERS, the continuity review's four questions); the
   funding rows for a paid Spanish review and teacher pilots stay, marked "only if funded".
4. **Lower layers are solid enough to build on, and expected to be revised** by what is built on them. No layer is
   declared finished in the abstract.
5. **What goes to Cory:** scope, audience, money, accounts, outside people, a live deploy, anything that moves a
   standard between sections, and anything this page cannot settle. Item-level choices inside the standards' limits
   are settled by the expert and reach Cory in a weekly digest (BASELINE, "What goes to Cory").

## Open questions on this page

- Closed 2026-11-14: the device assumption for kindergarten to grade 2 (section 5).
- Closed 2026-11-11: what happens to existing tools and specs that no section plan asks for (section 6, item 5).
- Closed 2026-11-24: "What a finished course holds" items. In: accessibility to a named level, a versioning and
  errata rule, a course teacher guide, a state alignment document. Spanish student materials after the core (L60).
  Not now: a print-volume count (docs/ROADMAP.md).
