al version made with a drawing or audio tool, the year's second kind of tool (W.1.5, W.1.6, review). Foundations block, new teaching: r-controlled ar and or, then er, ir and ur (cycles 19 and 20); the vowel teams oa, ai and ea, ay and ow, oo, ee and y, ie and igh (cycles 21 to 24), which complete RF.1.3c; two-syllable words with every syllable type (cycle 25); end-of-year fluency (e1-long-vowels and e1-syllables continued). Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.1.1a (unit 1); L.1.1j (unit 2). Spaced practice (Cory, 2026-11-24): two more touches of e1-word-relationships (L.1.5, L.1.6) on the unit's texts, spaced from unit 3's, and sentence conventions (e1-sentences) practiced in the opinion writing; review, not new ownership. | The base text's module 4. Author reasons come before the opinion piece in the unit because the opinion uses the claim-evidence-reasoning model; comparing needs two familiar texts, which units 3 and 4 give. The long text comes at the end of the year because students can now follow chapters and reread parts on their own, and it sits in module 4 unit 1's lessons in place of their trade books, so it adds no days to a unit with 38 days for 36 base lessons. Grammar is checked here after a year of use in talk and writing. | Candidate: the base text's module 4 unit assessments, adapted (comparing two bird stories; the nest opinion piece; an author's reasons and word strategies; the bird portrait) and the cycle 19 to 25 pattern record and the cumulative course-level record on 158 to 159, on all grade 1 patterns including vowel teams; collected once, with recent evidence reused. | An injured bird may upset a child: the story ends with the bird in care, and the text says children call an adult and never pick up a wild bird. Homes differ: no child is asked about a yard or feeder; any project is a class one. The true account names the group's place without picturing it as poor. |


