| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Sun, moon and stars. *Why is our shadow stick's shadow on one side in the morning and the other side after lunch?* Anchor: a pencil-in-clay shadow stick on the classroom windowsill or schoolyard, marked three times in one day | 8 | s1-sky-patterns | 1-ESS1-1 | Practice: making and using observations to describe patterns. Crosscutting: patterns | The sun seems to rise on one side of the sky, move across it and set; its place marked from one window or by a shadow stick at three times of day, over several days, so the class can predict where it will be. The moon seems to rise, move across the sky and set too (a moon drawing log at school, when it shows in the day sky, and at home). Stars other than the sun are seen at night and not in the day (the clarification's three parts). Star patterns only as seen at night; no Earth turning or orbit (grade 5). The meeting after this unit is unit 8's first day, counted only there: the class starts the daylight chart (light or dark at about 5:30 in the evening, and the first day strip) | First because the sun's daily path is the easiest pattern to watch in early fall, it reuses the kindergarten weather chart habit straight away, and it starts the daylight record that unit 8 and social studies grade 1 (from about week 4) both need | Candidate: the moon also seen in the day sky some mornings, as evidence that "the moon is only at night" is not right, checked in the unit plan | Never look straight at the sun: the sun's place is found by shadows. The home moon log is optional and never graded; families who cannot do it use the class's photo log. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | Parts that help. *How does a tree stay standing in a storm, and can we copy it to keep our tower from tipping?* Anchor: a photograph of a tall tree standing after a windstorm beside a tall paper tower that keeps falling over | 12 | s1-parts-mimic; s1-sketch-shape | 1-LS1-1; K-2-ETS1-2 | Practices: obtaining information from observations and pictures; developing a model (the sketch); designing solutions. Crosscutting: structure and function (introduced); systems | 1-LS1-1, both moves. First the core idea: plants and animals have outside parts that help them survive, grow and meet their needs (roots take in water and hold the plant, leaves face the light, a turtle's shell protects it, a hawk's eyes find food), and animals sense and respond to what is around them; plants respond too (a dated photograph sequence of a young plant's shoot turning toward the window light: what changed around the plant and how it responded, added to the chart and revisited in a short check; observation only, no mechanism); a part-and-job chart from real plants, pictures and video. Then the design: use materials to solve a human problem by copying how a plant or animal uses its parts. All four kinds in the clarification are shown as examples (protection like a turtle shell or acorn cap, a stand that will not tip like roots or a tail, keeping out like thorns, detecting like big ears or eyes); the class problem is the tower that tips, and groups choose one more. K-2-ETS1-2 in full: each group draws a simple labeled sketch or builds a physical model showing how the shape helps it work, and uses it to explain the design to others. The design cycle grows a step: problem, sketch, build, test | Second because it opens with retrieval of kindergarten's needs and system model, and it introduces structure-function and the planned sketch that unit 6 needs. Fall suits it: leaves and roots are easy to find outside. sk-define-problem from kindergarten is used here as review | Candidate: a second animal example of sensing and responding first hand (a class pill bug or snail moving away from light, if allowed), checked in the unit plan | No handling of wild animals; plant parts are never tasted. Craft tools child-safe. The human problems are general (a bike rider's head, a tower), never a child's own disability or need. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | Like their parents. *Are the little trees under our big maple like it? Exactly like it?* Anchor: the young maple (or oak) seedlings under a big tree in or near the schoolyard, with leaves pressed in October (during unit 2's outdoor looks) from the big tree and from two or three other trees of the same kind | 6 | s1-like-parents | 1-LS3-1 | Practices: making observations; constructing an evidence-based account. Crosscutting: patterns | Young plants and animals are like, but not exactly like, their parents. Plants first, and required: students line up leaves from the seedlings and from the parent tree by length and compare their shape, naming one feature the young share with the parent and one difference (where no seedlings grow, dated photographs of a parent plant and young plants grown from its seeds stand in); leaves from other trees of the same kind show the shape the kind shares. Then animals: young animals look like their parents but not exactly (photograph sets of a dog and her puppies, a cat and kittens, a duck and ducklings). The claim now rests on listed or ordered differences, and the class account covers plants and animals both; the unit's end task uses a fresh plant pair and a fresh animal pair. No inheritance or genes, no predicting, no hybrids, no animals that change form | After unit 2 because it uses the parts students just learned to name (leaf shape, ear shape, fur) as the features they compare, and the leaves pressed in October are ready when it opens (weeks 9 to 12). The first claim of the year, before unit 5's written account | Candidate: a class bean plant grown from a parent plant's seed, beside the parent, checked in the unit plan | Do not ask children to compare themselves with their parents (adoption, foster care, family separation); plants and animals only. The sensitivity agent studies adoptive, foster and separated families' own voice first (brief). If a child talks about their own family, the teacher listens, says one warm line ("Families are made in many ways, and every family is a real family"), asks nothing more, compares no one, and goes back to the plant or animal on the page. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | Sound. *Why does the rubber band go blurry when our box guitar plays, and why does the sound stop when we touch it?* Anchor: a shoebox guitar of stretched rubber bands | 7 | s1-sound-vibrates | 1-PS4-1 | Practice: planning and carrying out investigations with peers (saying what to do and what to watch for). Crosscutting: cause and effect | Vibrating materials make sound, and sound can make materials vibrate (both halves of the expectation): pluck a stretched rubber band, tap a drum with rice on top, touch a humming throat, hold paper near a speaker playing music, set rice on plastic wrap stretched over a bowl beside the speaker (a second case for the half children find harder: sound makes materials vibrate), touch a tuning fork (or a struck metal spoon) to water. A push or pluck sets the material moving back and forth, and that motion causes the sound. Planning grows from kindergarten: students now say what they will do and what they will watch for | Opens the physical science half of the year with retrieval of kindergarten's pushes and pulls, which it builds on. Before unit 5 because the two senses are taught one at a time before unit 6 uses both | Candidate: a string instrument or a phone speaker as a second source, and the stopping-the-sound test as evidence for the cause, checked in the unit plan | Sounds kept at classroom level and never near ears. Rubber bands held so they cannot snap toward faces; eye protection if anything can fly. No wave model (grade 4). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | Light. *Why can't we see the toy inside the box until we open the flap, and why does a beam show a bright patch behind wax paper but a shadow behind cardboard?* Anchor: a shoebox with a peephole and a toy inside, flap shut then open | 10 | s1-see-with-light; s1-light-materials | 1-PS4-2; 1-PS4-3 | Practices: making observations and planning investigations; constructing an evidence-based account. Crosscutting: cause and effect | We see objects only when light shines on them from a source (the sun, a lamp, a flashlight) or when the object gives off its own light, from a peephole box, a darkened room and a public-domain video of a cave explorer with a flashlight (the clarification's three kinds of observation); the account written with a sentence frame. Then an investigation of objects of different materials in a beam of light: clear plastic lets light through, wax paper some, cardboard blocks it and makes a shadow, a mirror sends it a new way (all four clarification kinds); students choose the materials and keep the flashlight in one place, then sort by how much light gets through, all, some or none (three groups), and count each; then they ask a second yes-or-no question of each object: does it send the light a new way? The mirror (and kitchen foil) says yes. No light entering the eye or reflection model (grade 4), no speed of light | After unit 4 so sound and light are each taught before unit 6 asks students to send a message with one. Seeing comes before materials because the material test needs the idea that light must reach an object. Winter's dark afternoons help the dark-room work. | Candidate: shadows of the same object made longer and shorter as a bridge to grade 5, checked in the unit plan for the boundary | Never look at the sun or shine a light into anyone's eyes. Some children fear the dark: they may hold the flashlight or sit by the door. Mirrors plastic or edge-taped; no lasers. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | Sending a message. *How can we tell a friend across the playground it is time to come in, without shouting?* Design problem: a message that must cross the playground or a long hallway Anchor: one ring of the school bell or one whistle at the end of recess calls the whole playground in at once, with no one shouting; design problem: build a light or sound signal that tells a friend across the playground or a long hallway it is time to come in | 8 | s1-signal-device | 1-PS4-4 | Practices: designing solutions; using measurement (length units). Crosscutting: cause and effect; structure and function (review) | Use tools and materials to design and build a device that uses light or sound to send a message across a distance: a cup-and-string telephone, a flashlight blink code, a pattern of beats on a box (the clarification's three kinds). Students define the problem, sketch (unit 2's skill, as review), build and test whether the message got through at a longer distance, measuring how far in same-size length units. No technical details of how phones or radios work | After units 4 and 5 because the device uses this year's sound and light ideas, and after unit 2 because it uses the sketch and the grown design cycle. Late winter, indoors in a hallway if the playground is cold | Candidate: two groups' devices tried at the same distance, as a first look at grade 2's comparison (not owned here), checked in the unit plan | String under tension at face height is a trip and eye hazard: keep it low and walk around it. Flashlights never pointed at eyes. Code signals avoid any pattern that could be mistaken for a real emergency signal. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). Anchor source: our own; a sound signal people use to share news across a distance is the idea of OpenSciEd elementary unit 1.2, Waves: Sound (a clock tower's bell; reference only, read 2026-11-14 at openscied.org; no wording used). |
| 7 | Parents and young. *Why does a baby bird cheep with its mouth open when its parent lands on the nest?* Anchor: a public-domain video of an American robin nest at feeding time | 6 | s1-parent-offspring | 1-LS1-2 | Practice: obtaining information from texts and media, comparing two sources. Crosscutting: patterns | Read texts and use media (Curio Class's own captioned pages and read-alouds, public-domain video) to find patterns in how parents and young behave to help the young survive: the young signal (crying, cheeping, calling) and the parents respond (feeding, comforting, carrying, protecting), the clarification's examples. Students compare text and media for each animal case: the American robin nest at feeding time and a white-tailed deer doe with her fawn (a bird and a mammal, both in public-domain federal video and photographs). Retrieve unit 3's parent-plant and young masters (invented for practice; real dated class records come at the media stage); a short caption establishes that adult plants as well as animals produce young. The assessment includes a fresh plant-and-young information prompt, without attributing animal-style care to plants | Spring, when young animals are born and nests are easy to watch. After unit 2 because "animals sense and respond" was taught there; the reading habit grows from kindergarten's forecast questions | Candidate: a schoolyard nest watched from a distance, if there is one, checked in the unit plan | Families differ (one parent, grandparents, foster and adoptive families): keep examples to plants and animals and never ask a child about their own family. No disturbing a real nest. The sensitivity agent studies adoptive, foster and separated families' own voice first (brief). If a child talks about their own family, the teacher listens, says one warm line ("Families are made in many ways, and every family is a real family"), asks nothing more, compares no one, and goes back to the plant or animal on the page. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 8 | Daylight and the seasons. *Is it light or dark at 5:30 in the evening, and will spring be like fall or like winter?* Anchor: the class's own daylight chart, started on day 9, just after unit 1, and marked each month from optional family reports, beside its monthly day strips | 5 | s1-daylight-seasons | 1-ESS1-2 | Practices: making observations at different times of year; analysing a chart. Crosscutting: patterns | Observations at the same clock time (about 5:30 in the evening, light or dark, from family reports) through fall, winter and spring, on the chart the class has kept, beside the monthly day strips (paper bars shaded light and dark from the town's public sunrise and sunset table, compared by length only); the class compares the categories and finds that winter has less daylight than fall or spring (the expectation's emphasis, relative comparison only, no counting hours). One day in fall (end of unit 1) starts the chart; four days in spring read and explain it | Last because it needs the whole year's record (the row says the unit opens in fall and closes in spring). It closes the year by returning to unit 1's sky. | Candidate: a photograph taken from the classroom window at the same clock time each month, as a second record, checked in the unit plan | Never look at the sun. Family reports are optional and never graded; two or three a month are enough, and the day strip carries the record in a month with none. Children are not asked about their evenings beyond light or dark. A school whose going-home time does cross sunset during the year may observe then instead. Agent reads: sensitivity. |


