{'strand': 'life', 'grade': '1', 'skill_id': 's1-parts-mimic', 'standard': '1-LS1-1', 'assumes': 'sk-living-needs; sk-needs-places; sk-define-problem', 'hands_on': 's2-habitat-diversity; s2-seed-pollinator-model; s3-variation-advantage; s4-structures-function; s4-senses-brain; s2-plant-needs-test', 'model_in': 'system-model; patterns; design-cycle', 'model_out': 'system-model; patterns; design-cycle; structure-function', 'words': 'root; stem; leaf; flower; fruit; shell; claw; part; job; mimic; protect', 'cross_subject': 'ela:RI.1.2 (find the main topic and key details in an informational text)', 'notes': "Two moves in one expectation. First the core idea: plants and animals have outside parts that help them survive, grow and meet their needs (roots take in water and hold the plant, leaves face the light, a turtle's shell protects it, a hawk's eyes find food), and animals sense and respond to what is around them; plants respond too (a dated photograph sequence of a young plant's shoot turning toward the light: what changed around the plant and how it responded; observation only, no mechanism). Students observe real plants, pictures and video and record part and job in a two-column chart. This builds on sk-living-needs (what living things need) and sk-needs-places (the system-model): each living thing is itself made of parts that work together. Introduces structure-function: the shape of a part fits its job. Then the design that completes 1-LS1-1: use materials to solve a human problem by copying how a plant or animal uses its parts (a helmet like a turtle shell or acorn cap, a stand that will not tip like roots or a tail, a fence with spikes like thorns, an alarm that hears like big ears). Students define the human problem (sk-define-problem) and build; the planned sketch is the work of s1-sketch-shape. Teacher note: no handling of wild animals; plant parts are not tasted. Retrieves the plant needs of sk-living-needs (roots take in water, leaves face the light) and hands them to s2-plant-needs-test, which tests them."}
{'strand': 'life', 'grade': '1', 'skill_id': 's1-parent-offspring', 'standard': '1-LS1-2', 'assumes': 'sk-forecast-severe', 'hands_on': 's2-fast-slow-events; s2-water-where; s3-animal-groups; s7-interaction-patterns', 'model_in': 'patterns', 'model_out': 'patterns', 'words': 'parent; offspring; young; protect; feed; signal; cry', 'cross_subject': 'ela:RI.1.1 (ask and answer questions about key details in a text)', 'notes': 'Read texts and use media (picture books, short video) to find patterns in how parents and young behave to help the young survive: the young signal (crying, cheeping, calling) and parents respond (feeding, comforting, carrying, protecting). The obtaining-information practice grows from sk-forecast-severe: now students read and compare more than one source for the same pattern. Teacher note: families differ (one parent, grandparents, foster and adoptive families); keep examples to animals and never ask a child to describe their own family. Also hands on to s7-interaction-patterns, where behaviour patterns return as interactions between populations.'}
{'strand': 'earth-space', 'grade': '1', 'skill_id': 's1-sky-patterns', 'standard': '1-ESS1-1', 'assumes': 'sk-weather-watch', 'hands_on': 's3-motion-patterns; s5-shadows-sky', 'model_in': 'patterns', 'model_out': 'patterns', 'words': 'sun; moon; star; rise; set; sky; day; night', 'cross_subject': 'ela:W.1.8 (recall observations to answer a question)', 'notes': "Use observations of the sun, moon and stars to describe patterns that can be predicted: the sun and moon seem to rise on one side of the sky, move across it and set; stars other than the sun are seen at night and not in the day. Students mark the sun's place from one window or a shadow stick at three times of day and keep a moon drawing log at home and school, extending the daily record habit of sk-weather-watch. Boundary: star patterns only as seen at night, not in the day; no Earth turning or orbit model (that is grade 5). Teacher note: never look straight at the sun; observe its position by shadows."}
