5. `## Units`: one table, columns exactly
   `| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |`
   For ELA, replace C3 with Texts in this header; for science, head the C3 column Practices and crosscutting
   concepts.
   - **Skills:** the skill_id of every progression row the unit teaches, `; `-separated. Every row the course owns
     sits in exactly one unit. A unit that revisits an earlier row's skill names it in the text as review, never in
     this column.
   - **Owned standards:** the codes those rows own, as written in the rows.
   - **Why this unit, here:** why it is a unit and why it sits at this point (order of time, a skill or model it
     needs from an earlier unit, a seam).
   - **What students learn:** a lettered part of an owned standard (RL.4.1a, MA.WHII.T1.1a) is named by its code in
     the unit that teaches it, so the gate can see every part placed (standards in full).
   - Units in an order that uses a skill or model before it is taught fail the read.
6. `## Course-level checks`: one table, columns exactly
   `| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |`, one row per check (mid-year, year-end), each
   with at least one respond day. A row with "review" in its Check cell holds review days (Check days counts them,
   Respond days is 0). In K to 8, place the checks on the target weeks of docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, so no week (K to 5)
   or day (6 to 8) holds two subjects' checks.
7. `## Cross-subject`: every row's cross_subject lean, grouped by the other subject and grade: what this course
   needs, and when in this course's year. Same-year leans form the availability calendar: which unit of this
   course uses it and the earliest unit of the other course that may teach it, so the other subject's frame can
   honour it. Where the other subject's frame is not written yet, record the need and do not guess its unit
   numbers. A citation of another frame's unit gives its course days as that frame states them
   ("math grade 4 unit 3 (days 30 to 47)"); the gate notes a citation more than 3 days off that unit's place in
   data/plans/calendar.csv.
8. `## For the lead`: anything that would change what a grade teaches beyond what the decisions settle, with your
   pick. Scope, money, people or audience questions go here, never decided by you.

## Lessons from the frame reads
These faults came back course after course in the expert and Codex reads of the 15 social studies frames
(docs/reviews/frame-*-fixes-2026-11-13.md). Write so none of them comes back. `scripts/frame.py` checks lessons 1, 2,
3, 4 (lettered parts, a note), 12 and 14, the one Course-level checks table, the Seams, Models carried and
Cross-subject ids, and outside-reader wording:
1. **A respond day after each course-level check,** counted in the course's days.
2. **Check-set cells start "Candidate: ...":** the unit plan settles them.
3. **The time a day is stated** (minutes per lesson, lessons a week), and why the day total fits it.
4. **Standards in full.** Every lettered part and every clarification a standard names is taught somewhere, and the
   unit that teaches it says so. A standard is never quietly narrowed to its headline.
5. **Each thread starts where it is first used.** A readiness check or retrieval task (lesson 16) on an earlier
   grade's skill sits in the first unit that needs it; a model is introduced before any unit uses it.
6. **"First" claims are checked against the grade before** ("the first full sourcing step", "introduces"). One
   thread keeps one wording across grades.
7. **Both ends of every handoff.** Where the frame needs a link the progression lacks, write it under "For the
   lead" with the row change; the fixer edits both rows.
8. **Same-year cross-subject leans carry an "if later" line:** what the unit does if the other subject has not
   taught it yet (for example, the unit teaches the percent it needs itself).
9. **Days by load.** Weigh each unit's rows and notes; the readers named units short for what they own.
10. **Questions answerable from the unit.** A unit's compelling or driving question can be answered from the
    evidence the unit itself studies.

20. **Lean only on core courses.**
    (a) Same-year leans need an "if later" line; an elective cannot assume another elective is taken.
    (b) sequence-physics "Across the band" 5 (math leans needed one rule, now sequence-write rule 2); psychology draft
    leans on "Algebra I and II statistics" (core, fine).
    (c) Cross-subject leans name core courses only; a lean on another elective is a bridge the unit teaches.
    (d) All.

## Already covered by frame-write.md

- Respond day after each check (lesson 1); minutes and lessons a week (3); standards in full (4); readiness timing,
  unit 1 on day 1 after the hook (16); sensitivity reads line (12); no math ahead of math (13); paper path bullet (14);
  stale status lines (15); "Candidate:" cells (2); days by load (9). Each held below the frame; item 2 above adds only
  the evidence and counting-grade part.
- Mid-unit assessment in units over 25 days: in the Days bullet wording.
- Year-end direct fluency station: sequence-write rule 2; the three elective math drafts already carry "Fluency without
  a timer" bullets.
- Collect once, class size 30, "Our words" for days.csv: sequence-write rule 2 (lesson 16 above extends "Our words" to
  the frame's Source map).
- A pilot course per new subject, gated layouts, rule 2 count at scope stage: PRODUCTION-PATH "Before a new subject's
  scopes"; not frame text.
- Same-period scoring and error-letter rules: UNIT-PLAN fields 5 and 6 and the lens brief; not frame matters.

## Brief gaps (proposed lessons 17 to 27 for frame-write.md)

17. **Day kinds defined.** A "Day kinds" bullet: first explicit teaching is Understanding; Fluency practises only what
    an earlier day taught; investigating, drafting, editing or a new task is Application. Say what Fluency means in
    this course.
18. **Readiness evidence, not verdicts.** Each assumed row gives its last-use grade and core day; the treatment follows
    the count, which the scope gate governs. No "kept in use" without a day. A course open to two grades names the
    grade the count starts from.
19. **Scored evidence placed.** Each unit names its end-of-unit evidence form; a product's draft, feedback and revision
    days are counted in the unit's days; no score from a first-teaching day; end days hold one full performance each.
20. **Checks claim samples.** "What it checks" names the units sampled, the task forms and any record reused unscored,
    never "every code".
21. **Inside the period.** Set-up, clean-up and collection fit the lesson's minutes; readiness is ten minutes.
22. **Safety named.** The Safety read bullet names guides by title and free link (never a student form); the last
    Units column gives each hands-on or field unit's hazards and controls, in the words days.csv will use.
23. **Facts sourced.** Anchors, cases, figures and data sets carry a free source, an "as of" date where they can change,
    or "invented for practice".
